import Foundation

enum PD1QuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A new Apex developer asks why their code sharing the org with hundreds of other customers must obey strict per-transaction limits on SOQL queries and DML statements. What concept explains this, and what is the underlying mechanism?", options: [("A", "Governor limits exist solely to encourage customers to purchase additional API call add-ons and have no relationship to shared infrastructure."), ("B", "Multi-tenancy: many customer orgs share the same physical infrastructure and application layer, so governor limits are hard, per-transaction enforcement caps that prevent one tenant's runaway code from starving shared resources for everyone else."), ("C", "Governor limits are configurable per org and can be disabled entirely by a System Administrator for high-usage customers."), ("D", "Single-tenancy: each customer org gets fully dedicated hardware, so governor limits exist only as a historical artifact with no current technical purpose.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce's multi-tenant architecture means many organizations share the same underlying servers and database; governor limits are the platform's hard enforcement mechanism ensuring no single tenant's code can monopolize shared resources and degrade the experience for other tenants. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Governor limits are a core architectural safeguard tied directly to shared infrastructure protection, not a monetization mechanism for upsells.  \n* C: Governor limits are hard platform enforcement that cannot be disabled by an admin; they are fixed based on context (synchronous vs. asynchronous) regardless of edition or usage level.  \n* D: Salesforce is explicitly multi-tenant, not single-tenant — dedicated hardware per customer is not how the platform is architected, and this contradicts the very reason governor limits exist."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "A developer is structuring a new Lightning Web Component feature and wants to align with the Model-View-Controller pattern already implicit in Salesforce's architecture. Which mapping is correct? (Choose 1)", options: [("A", "Model = sObjects and fields (the data layer); View = Visualforce pages and Lightning Web Components (the presentation layer); Controller = Apex classes, triggers, and Flow (the logic layer)."), ("B", "Model = Apex triggers; View = governor limits; Controller = SOQL queries."), ("C", "There is no MVC mapping in Salesforce; the platform uses a purely event-driven architecture with no separation of concerns."), ("D", "Model = Visualforce page; View = sObjects and fields; Controller = Lightning Web Components.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: This mapping correctly separates the three MVC layers as they exist on the Salesforce platform — sObjects/fields hold data (Model), Apex/triggers/Flow manipulate that data (Controller), and Visualforce/LWC present it to users (View). Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Governor limits and SOQL queries are platform mechanisms and data-access statements, not MVC layers at all; this option misapplies unrelated concepts to the MVC pattern.  \n* C: Salesforce's architecture does implement a clear separation of concerns matching MVC; claiming no such mapping exists is inaccurate.  \n* D: This reverses the actual Model and Controller/View roles entirely — Visualforce is a View technology and LWC is also a View technology, not a Controller."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "Choose 2. A team is scoping a new feature request: display a real-time calculated \"Days Since Last Contact\" value on the Account record, computed from a related Task's ActivityDate, with no persistence needed and no cross-object DML. Which two facts correctly justify recommending a formula field over Apex?", options: [("A", "Formula fields can perform DML on related child records, making them equally capable as an Apex trigger for this scenario."), ("B", "Formula fields are read-only, evaluate in real time, and can reference fields on the record or (within traversal limits) a parent object without requiring any code or test coverage."), ("C", "Formula fields can loop over collections of related records to compute complex conditional aggregates."), ("D", "Formula fields require full Apex test coverage before deployment, identical to a trigger-based solution."), ("E", "Since no persistence or cross-object DML is required, a formula field's real-time, no-code evaluation fully satisfies the requirement without the maintenance overhead of Apex.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Why correct: Formula fields are the correct tool here because they evaluate in real time with no code or tests, and this scenario's requirements (no persistence, no cross-object DML) sit squarely within a formula field's capabilities. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Formula fields are strictly read-only and can never perform DML of any kind — this is one of their defining limitations, not a capability.  \n* C: Formula fields cannot loop over collections or process child records with conditional logic — that requires Apex or a Roll-Up Summary field for simple aggregates.  \n* D: Formula fields require zero Apex test coverage since they aren't Apex at all; this is actually one of their advantages over a trigger-based approach."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "A roll-up summary field is needed to total the Amount of all child Opportunity Line Items on a parent Opportunity, but the relationship between the two objects is a standard lookup, not master-detail. What must the architect understand about this limitation?", options: [("A", "True declarative roll-up summary fields are exclusively available on master-detail relationships; since the relationship here is a lookup, the team must use an alternative such as Apex, Flow-based aggregation, or a scheduled job to calculate and store the total."), ("B", "Roll-up summary fields work identically regardless of relationship type, so no workaround is needed."), ("C", "The lookup relationship must be entirely removed and replaced with a formula field to achieve the same aggregation."), ("D", "Roll-up summary fields can be created on lookup relationships as long as the child object has fewer than 200 records.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Roll-up summary fields are a master-detail-exclusive declarative feature; when only a lookup relationship exists, the team must implement an alternative mechanism (Apex trigger, Flow with Get Records and aggregation logic, or scheduled batch) to achieve the equivalent aggregate calculation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This is factually incorrect — declarative roll-up summary fields simply do not function on lookup relationships regardless of configuration attempts.  \n* C: A formula field cannot aggregate multiple child records' values into a single sum on the parent; formulas operate on a single record's own fields or direct parent traversal, not child aggregation.  \n* D: There is no record-count threshold that enables roll-up summary fields on lookup relationships; the master-detail requirement is absolute and unrelated to child record volume."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "Choose 2. A developer is evaluating when declarative automation (Flow) is preferable to Apex for a new approval-adjacent business process. Which two considerations correctly favor Flow over Apex in this decision?", options: [("A", "The logic can be expressed with simple to moderate branching, field updates, and record creation, and needs to be maintained by non-developer admins without deployment cycles."), ("B", "The process requires complex nested loops processing tens of thousands of records with tight governor-limit-sensitive bulk logic."), ("C", "The process requires implementing a custom Batchable interface with stateful accumulation across execute() calls."), ("D", "The logic is straightforward enough that expressing it declaratively avoids the overhead of writing and maintaining Apex test classes."), ("E", "The process needs an external HTTP callout with custom retry and backoff logic on failure.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Flow is the right choice when the logic is simple-to-moderate, needs ongoing maintenance by admins without a full deployment cycle, and when avoiding Apex's mandatory test-class overhead is a genuine benefit for straightforward logic. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Complex, high-volume, governor-limit-sensitive bulk processing exceeds Flow's practical capabilities and points toward Apex (often Batch Apex) instead.  \n* C: Implementing the Batchable interface with Database.Stateful is an Apex-only construct; this consideration would favor Apex, not Flow.  \n* E: Custom retry/backoff logic on HTTP callouts requires the fine-grained control only available in Apex; Flow's callout capabilities are far more limited."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "A validation rule already blocks Opportunity saves when Amount is negative. A developer proposes replacing it with an Apex trigger doing the same check, arguing the trigger is \"more powerful.\" What should be considered about the order of execution and best practice here?", options: [("A", "Validation rules cannot block a record save under any circumstances; only Apex triggers can prevent a DML operation from completing."), ("B", "Apex triggers always run before validation rules in the save order, so switching to Apex would catch the error earlier and is strictly superior."), ("C", "There is no meaningful difference; validation rules and Apex triggers are functionally and architecturally identical constructs."), ("D", "Since validation rules run early in the save order (before Apex triggers) and require no code or tests, replacing this simple, single-field check with an Apex trigger adds unnecessary maintenance overhead with no functional benefit.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Validation rules run early in the save order (before triggers), require no code, and are simpler to maintain for straightforward field-level checks — replacing this with Apex would add code, tests, and deployment overhead for zero functional gain. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Validation rules are specifically designed to block a save by returning an error when their formula evaluates to true; this is their core, defining capability.  \n* B: This reverses the actual order of execution — while system validation rules (required fields, field length, data type checks) do run before Apex before-triggers, custom validation rules specifically run AFTER before-triggers (and before after-triggers), not before them; this option's blanket claim about \"validation rules\" firing before triggers is only true for system validation, not custom validation rules.  \n* C: Validation rules are declarative, no-code constructs, while Apex triggers are code-based — they are architecturally distinct with different capabilities, trade-offs, and points in the save order (custom validation rules run after before-triggers, not before them)."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "A batch job processing 10,000 Contact records in a single synchronous Apex transaction attempts to issue one SOQL query per record inside a loop, and separately issues one DML update per record inside the same loop. Which governor limits are at risk, and in what order will they likely be hit?", options: [("A", "The org-wide 24-hour API call limit will be exceeded first, before either the SOQL or DML per-transaction limit is reached."), ("B", "Only the DML limit matters because SOQL queries inside a loop are automatically deduplicated by the query optimizer."), ("C", "The 100 SOQL-queries-per-synchronous-transaction limit will be exceeded well before the 150 DML-statements limit, since both counters increment once per loop iteration but the SOQL cap (100) is lower than the DML cap (150)."), ("D", "Only the DML statement limit (150) is at risk; SOQL queries have no meaningful per-transaction cap."), ("E", "Neither limit is at risk because Salesforce automatically batches queries and DML issued inside loops into a single call behind the scenes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: With one SOQL query and one DML statement per loop iteration, the SOQL counter reaches its synchronous cap of 100 well before the DML counter reaches its cap of 150, since both increment at the same rate but the SOQL ceiling is lower — the transaction throws an uncatchable LimitException on the 101st query. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The 24-hour API limit governs external API request volume across the org over a rolling day, and is unrelated to same-transaction Apex governor limits like per-transaction SOQL/DML counts.  \n* B: There is no automatic deduplication of SOQL queries inside a loop; each iteration issues a distinct query that counts individually against the limit.  \n* D: SOQL queries absolutely have a hard per-transaction cap (100 synchronous) — this is one of Apex's most fundamental and well-known limits.  \n* E: Salesforce does not automatically batch or bulkify queries or DML placed inside a loop; each iteration executes its own separate query/DML statement, which is precisely the well-documented anti-pattern this scenario illustrates."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "A developer needs a field on a custom object that will be used as the match key for an integration's upsert operations from an external inventory system, ensuring no duplicate inventory records are created across repeated sync runs. What field configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "A Formula field referencing the record's own Salesforce Id, since Salesforce Ids are always unique."), ("B", "A custom Text field with both the \"External ID\" and \"Unique\" checkboxes enabled, providing an indexed, duplicate-preventing key recognized by upsert operations for matching against the external system's own identifier."), ("C", "A Picklist field manually populated with every known external system identifier value."), ("D", "A standard Auto Number field, since Salesforce auto numbers are always guaranteed to match values coming from any external system.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A Text field marked as both External ID and Unique is indexed for fast lookup and enforces no-duplicate values, exactly matching the standard configuration needed for an upsert match key tied to an external system's own identifier. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A formula field referencing the Salesforce record Id is read-only and only exists once the record is already created in Salesforce; it cannot serve as an externally-supplied match key used to decide whether to insert or update during an upsert.  \n* C: Auto Number fields are generated sequentially and internally by Salesforce; they cannot be set to match or align with an external system's independently generated identifier values.  \n* D: A static picklist of known identifiers cannot scale as new inventory records are created externally, and picklists are not designed to serve as unique, indexed upsert match keys."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "Choose 2. A developer is writing Apex that needs to traverse from a custom object \"Service_Request__c\" up to its parent \"Account\" and further up to the Account Owner's Name, entirely through relationship dot-notation in a single SOQL query. Which two facts about relationship traversal are accurate?", options: [("A", "SOQL relationship traversal has no depth limit; queries can traverse an unlimited number of parent relationship levels."), ("B", "The __r suffix must be used for both custom and standard relationships without exception."), ("C", "Custom lookup or master-detail relationships require the __r suffix when traversed in SOQL (e.g., Service_Request__c.Account__r.Owner.Name), while standard relationships like Account.Owner do not use this suffix."), ("D", "SOQL supports traversing parent relationships up to a maximum of 5 levels deep in a single query."), ("E", "Traversing more than 2 relationship levels always requires switching to two separate SOQL queries joined in Apex.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Custom relationships use the __r suffix specifically (standard relationships do not), and Salesforce enforces a documented maximum of 5 levels of relationship traversal in a single SOQL query — both facts are accurate, well-established SOQL rules. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This directly contradicts the documented 5-level traversal limit; SOQL relationship traversal is not unlimited.  \n* B: The __r suffix is specifically for custom relationships; standard relationships (like Account.Owner or Contact.Account) use the relationship name directly without any suffix.  \n* E: Traversal up to 5 levels can be accomplished within a single SOQL query using continued dot notation; splitting into two queries is not required simply because more than 2 levels are involved, as long as the 5-level cap isn't exceeded."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "A developer wants to write a generic Apex utility method that accepts any sObject type and dynamically inspects its fields at runtime to determine which are picklists, without hardcoding object or field names. Which approach is correct?", options: [("A", "Use Schema.getGlobalDescribe() to get a Map of all SObjectTypes, then call getDescribe() on the target type to get a DescribeSObjectResult, then call getDescribe() on each field token from its fields map to get a DescribeFieldResult, checking getType() for Schema.DisplayType.PICKLIST."), ("B", "Use SOQL to query the FieldDefinition object at runtime, since this is the only way to determine field types dynamically in Apex."), ("C", "Hardcode a switch statement listing every possible field name across all objects the org might use, then manually check each against a fixed picklist name list."), ("D", "Dynamic field-type inspection is not possible in Apex under any circumstances; this always requires a metadata deployment tool instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The Schema namespace's getGlobalDescribe(), DescribeSObjectResult, and DescribeFieldResult classes are specifically designed for exactly this kind of dynamic, metadata-driven runtime inspection — retrieving field type information (including picklist detection via DisplayType.PICKLIST) without hardcoding object or field names. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: While FieldDefinition can be queried via SOQL/Tooling API in some contexts, this is not the standard, native Apex mechanism for this task — the Schema describe methods are the correct, documented approach available directly in Apex.  \n* C: Hardcoding every possible field name defeats the entire purpose of writing a generic, reusable utility method and would need to be manually updated whenever schema changes.  \n* D: Dynamic field-type inspection is a well-supported, common Apex capability via the Schema namespace; claiming it's impossible is factually incorrect."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "Choose 3. A developer is deciding on field types and Apex data type mappings while designing a new custom object. Which three field-type-to-Apex-type mappings are correct?", options: [("A", "Date/Time maps to Date, discarding the time component entirely."), ("B", "Lookup field maps to Id."), ("C", "Checkbox maps to Boolean."), ("D", "Currency maps to Decimal (never Double, to preserve precision for monetary values)."), ("E", "Text Area (Long) maps to Integer.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Checkbox correctly maps to Boolean, Currency correctly maps to Decimal (Double would risk floating-point precision loss for money), and Lookup fields correctly map to the 18-character Id type — all three are accurate, standard Salesforce-to-Apex type mappings. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Date/Time fields map to the Apex DateTime type, which preserves both date and time components; Date (without time) is a separate, distinct field type and Apex type.  \n* E: Text Area (Long) is a text-based field type and maps to String in Apex, not Integer, which is a numeric type entirely unrelated to long-form text storage."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "A developer needs to model a many-to-many relationship between Volunteer and Event custom objects, where each pairing should track its own hours logged and a check-in timestamp specific to that pairing. What is the correct data model, and why not a multi-select picklist?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field on Volunteer that lists all associated Events with their hours."), ("B", "A single master-detail relationship from Volunteer to Event, since one relationship field is sufficient to represent any many-to-many association."), ("C", "A single multi-select picklist field on Volunteer listing all Event names, since multi-select picklists can store additional attributes per selected value."), ("D", "A junction object (e.g., Volunteer_Event__c) with two master-detail relationships — one to Volunteer, one to Event — where each junction record independently stores Hours_Logged__c and Check_In_Time__c; a multi-select picklist cannot store per-pairing attributes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The junction object pattern with two master-detail relationships is the standard Salesforce approach for many-to-many relationships, and because each junction record is its own record, it can independently store pairing-specific attributes like hours logged and check-in time, which a multi-select picklist fundamentally cannot do. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values (SUM, COUNT, MIN, MAX) from master-detail children into a single number; they cannot list multiple related records with their individual attribute values.  \n* B: A single master-detail (or lookup) relationship field only supports a one-to-many relationship in one direction; it cannot represent a true many-to-many association where either side can relate to multiple records on the other side.  \n* C: A multi-select picklist can only store a list of selected text values; it has no capability to attach additional attributes (like hours or a timestamp) to each individual selected value."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "Choose 2. A developer is comparing the LWC and Aura component frameworks before starting a new UI project. Which two statements accurately describe the current guidance and technical differences?", options: [("A", "A valid reason to still touch Aura today is to extend or maintain existing Aura components that cannot practically be refactored, or when an Aura-only feature is specifically required."), ("B", "LWC components can only run inside Salesforce Classic, never Lightning Experience."), ("C", "Aura is the newer framework and is actively recommended over LWC for any new development due to superior performance."), ("D", "LWC is built on modern web standards (Custom Elements, Shadow DOM, ES modules), generally offers better performance, and is Salesforce's recommended framework for all new component development."), ("E", "Both frameworks are functionally and architecturally identical, so framework choice is purely a matter of personal taste.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Why correct: LWC's standards-based architecture generally delivers better performance and is Salesforce's clear recommendation for new development, while a legitimate reason to still work in Aura is maintaining or extending pre-existing Aura components that can't be practically migrated. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: LWC and Aura components both run exclusively in Lightning Experience; neither renders in Salesforce Classic, which uses the older Visualforce-based UI.  \n* C: This reverses the actual relationship — Aura is the older, legacy framework, and LWC is the newer, currently-recommended one.  \n* E: The two frameworks are architecturally distinct (different programming models, different performance characteristics, different underlying technology), not identical."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A team is planning to move org customizations (objects, fields, Apex, flows) from a sandbox to production through a CI/CD pipeline rather than manual configuration. What underlying Salesforce capability makes this portable and version-controllable?", options: [("A", "Chatter, since Chatter posts can be configured to track and replicate metadata changes across orgs."), ("B", "There is no way to represent Salesforce configuration as portable files; all configuration must be manually re-created in each environment."), ("C", "The Metadata API, which represents every org customization — objects, fields, page layouts, Apex classes, flows, permission sets — as XML files that can be packaged, retrieved, deployed, and version-controlled."), ("D", "The REST API, since it is the only Salesforce API capable of representing configuration as deployable files.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The Metadata API is specifically designed to represent org customizations as XML metadata files, making configuration portable across environments, deployable via CI/CD pipelines, and trackable in a version control system like Git. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Chatter is a collaboration and social feed feature; it has no relationship to metadata representation, packaging, or deployment.  \n* B: This is factually incorrect — the Metadata API's entire purpose is to make Salesforce configuration portable, deployable, and version-controllable, which is exactly what modern CI/CD pipelines rely on.  \n* D: The REST API is designed for querying and manipulating record data (not metadata/configuration) and is not the mechanism used for deploying org customizations between environments."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "Choose 2. A developer is building a Lightning Web Component that will be installed by other Salesforce customers via a managed package, and is concerned about Lightning Locker Service implications. Which two statements about Locker Service are accurate?", options: [("A", "Components can freely use document.querySelector() to reach into any other component's DOM without restriction under Locker Service."), ("B", "Global browser objects like document and window are proxied versions under Locker Service, which block certain potentially dangerous operations."), ("C", "Locker Service allows any component, regardless of namespace, unrestricted access to any other component's shadow DOM by design."), ("D", "Locker Service only applies to Aura components and has no effect on Lightning Web Components."), ("E", "Locker Service wraps each component's JavaScript in a strict-mode sandbox so that a component in one namespace cannot directly access the DOM of a component in a different namespace.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Why correct: Locker Service's core purpose is namespace isolation — preventing cross-namespace DOM access — and it proxies global browser objects like document and window to block dangerous operations, both of which are foundational, accurate descriptions of how Locker Service protects components from one another. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Using document.querySelector() to reach into another component's DOM violates Locker Service's isolation model and is specifically the kind of dangerous, cross-boundary access the sandbox is designed to block.  \n* C: This is the opposite of Locker Service's actual behavior — it specifically prevents unrestricted cross-namespace shadow DOM access, which is the whole point of the isolation mechanism.  \n* D: Locker Service (and its DOM/JS isolation principles) applies to both Aura and Lightning Web Components, not just one framework exclusively."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "A developer needs to decide whether a business requirement to \"round-robin assign new Leads to the next available rep in a rotating list, recalculating on the fly based on current workload\" should be built with a formula field, a roll-up summary field, or Apex. Which is correct, and why?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field, since roll-ups can assign owners to records based on aggregate child counts."), ("B", "A formula field, since formulas can perform round-robin assignment logic by referencing a counter field on a related object."), ("C", "A Picklist field with all rep names manually rotated by an admin each morning."), ("D", "Apex (likely a trigger or invocable method called from Flow), since this requires stateful, conditional logic across multiple records with a decision (which rep is \"next\") that a formula field or roll-up summary field cannot compute or persist as an assignment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Round-robin assignment requires evaluating current workload state across multiple rep records and making a decision that gets persisted as an OwnerId change — this kind of stateful, cross-record decision and write operation exceeds what formula fields (read-only, no DML) or roll-up summary fields (simple aggregation only) can do, making Apex (or Apex-backed Flow logic) the correct tool. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields only aggregate numeric values (SUM, COUNT, MIN, MAX) from master-detail child records; they have no capability to assign record ownership based on any logic.  \n* B: Formula fields are strictly read-only and cannot write an assignment decision to the OwnerId field, nor can they maintain cross-record rotation state.  \n* C: Manually rotating a picklist value each morning is not an automated, real-time round-robin assignment mechanism and does not scale or respond dynamically to current workload."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "Choose 2. A developer is scoping a new feature and needs to correctly identify the audience boundaries of the Platform Developer I credential relative to what a candidate is NOT expected to know. Which two are explicitly outside the expected scope for this exam's candidate profile?", options: [("A", "Administering standard Salesforce applications (e.g., configuring standard Sales Cloud settings as an admin task)."), ("B", "Writing basic Apex classes and triggers using object-oriented programming principles."), ("C", "Developing and publishing managed products on AppExchange."), ("D", "Familiarity with testing, debugging, and deploying Apex code."), ("E", "Understanding governor limits and their implications for Apex transactions.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: The exam guide explicitly states a candidate is not expected to administer standard Salesforce applications or develop and publish managed products on AppExchange — these are outside the defined scope of the Platform Developer I audience profile. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Writing Apex classes and triggers with object-oriented principles is squarely within the expected skill set of this exam's audience, not outside it.  \n* D: Familiarity with testing, debugging, and deployment is explicitly listed as part of the expected skill set for this credential.  \n* E: Understanding governor limits and their implications is explicitly listed as expected knowledge for this exam's candidate."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "A developer needs to programmatically determine, before attempting a DML operation, whether the running user has create access on the Account object without triggering an exception if they don't. What is the correct approach?", options: [("A", "Query the User object's Profile field and manually maintain a hardcoded list of profiles allowed to create Accounts."), ("B", "Call Schema.SObjectType.Account.isCreateable(), which returns a Boolean indicating whether the running user has object-level create permission, allowing the check to happen proactively before any DML is attempted."), ("C", "Attempt the DML operation inside a try-catch block and assume any exception thrown means the user lacks create access."), ("D", "Assume all users can create Accounts unless a validation rule explicitly blocks it.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Schema.SObjectType.Account.isCreateable() is the standard, documented method for checking object-level CRUD permissions proactively, returning a simple Boolean without needing to attempt (and potentially fail) an actual DML operation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Hardcoding a list of profiles is brittle, requires manual maintenance whenever profiles or permission sets change, and does not reflect the platform's actual permission model including permission set grants.  \n* C: Relying on catching an exception after attempting DML is a reactive, fragile pattern — it wastes a DML statement (counting against governor limits) and conflates different possible failure causes rather than checking permissions proactively.  \n* D: This assumption is incorrect and dangerous — object-level create access is governed by profile and permission set configuration, not by validation rules, which only apply after a create is already permitted and attempted."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "Choose 2. An architect is documenting the relationship between formula fields and roll-up summary fields for a new team of developers. Which two statements are accurate?", options: [("A", "Roll-up summary fields require a master-detail relationship between the parent and child objects."), ("B", "SUM, MIN, and MAX roll-up summary types require a numeric, currency, or date field on the child object to aggregate."), ("C", "Roll-up summary fields support COUNT, SUM, MIN, and MAX aggregate functions; AVG is not natively supported as a roll-up summary aggregate type."), ("D", "Formula fields can perform DML operations to update related records when their calculated value changes."), ("E", "Formula fields are always writable and can be directly edited by end users on a record's edit page.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: Roll-up summary fields indeed only support COUNT, SUM, MIN, and MAX (not AVG natively), and they require a master-detail relationship as their only supported configuration — both statements are accurate and commonly tested facts. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: While this statement is factually true about roll-up field requirements, it isn't paired here as one of the two most relevant contrasting facts being tested against the false statements in B and D — A and C represent the clearest, most fundamental accurate pairing for this question's intent.  \n* D: Formula fields cannot perform DML under any circumstances; they are purely computed, read-only display values with zero write capability.  \n* E: Formula fields are always read-only and can never be directly edited by end users on a record edit page; this is one of their defining, universal characteristics."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "A developer building an Agentforce-integrated feature needs to expose an Apex method as an agent action that retrieves a customer's order history. What annotation and design consideration applies?", options: [("A", "Apex methods cannot be exposed to Agentforce under any circumstances; only declarative Flow logic can be used as agent actions."), ("B", "The method should be annotated with @InvocableMethod, the same annotation used to expose methods to Flow, since Agentforce discovers and can invoke these same methods as agent actions."), ("C", "The method must be marked @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) exclusively, since this is the only annotation Agentforce recognizes."), ("D", "Agentforce requires a proprietary annotation, @AgentAction, that is entirely separate from and incompatible with Flow's invocable method system.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Any Apex method annotated with @InvocableMethod is automatically discoverable and usable as an agent action in Agentforce — this is the same annotation used to expose methods to Flow, meaning a single method can serve both Flow and Agentforce without additional special annotations. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This is factually incorrect — Apex methods are a supported and common way to expose deterministic actions to Agentforce agents via @InvocableMethod.  \n* C: @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) is specifically for exposing methods to LWC/Aura components, not Agentforce agent actions; it is an entirely different annotation serving a different integration surface.  \n* D: There is no separate, proprietary @AgentAction annotation — Agentforce reuses the existing @InvocableMethod mechanism that already exists for Flow integration."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "Choose 2. A developer is deciding whether Agentforce is appropriate for a new requirement: automatically approving or rejecting expense reports based on precise dollar-threshold business rules with guaranteed, auditable outcomes. Which two considerations correctly argue against using Agentforce for the core decision logic?", options: [("A", "Agentforce is exclusively for creating new records and cannot read any existing data."), ("B", "Agentforce cannot be integrated with Apex at all, so it is technically incapable of participating in this workflow in any capacity."), ("C", "LLM-based agents are inherently non-deterministic — the same input can produce different outputs across runs, which conflicts with the need for guaranteed, auditable, repeatable decisions."), ("D", "Agentforce always requires a human reviewer for every single action it takes, making it unusable in any automated approval context."), ("E", "Transactional, precise business-rule enforcement (like exact dollar thresholds) is better suited to deterministic Apex or declarative logic than to a generative AI agent's judgment.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 4], explanation: "Why correct: LLM-based agents are non-deterministic by nature, and precise, auditable, rule-based decisions (like exact dollar-threshold approvals) are better handled by deterministic Apex or declarative logic — both facts correctly explain why Agentforce shouldn't drive this specific transactional decision, even though it could still play a supporting role (e.g., summarizing or drafting communication). Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Agentforce is grounded in org data and can read existing records as part of its reasoning and action-taking; it is not limited to creating new records only.  \n* B: Agentforce integrates directly with Apex via @InvocableMethod-annotated agent actions; claiming zero integration capability is factually incorrect.  \n* D: Agentforce does not universally require human review for every action; while human-in-the-loop patterns exist, this is not a blanket, absolute requirement for all agent actions."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "A developer is deciding between a Text field and a Text Area (Long) field for storing a customer's detailed service history notes, expected to sometimes reach 3,000 characters. What consideration should guide this decision?", options: [("A", "Both field types have identical character limits, so the choice is purely aesthetic."), ("B", "A standard Text field has no character limit and should always be preferred for any text content regardless of length."), ("C", "Text Area (Long) fields cannot be referenced in Apex and are therefore unusable for this scenario."), ("D", "A standard Text field caps out at 255 characters, far short of the expected 3,000-character need, while Text Area (Long) supports up to 131,072 characters, making it the appropriate choice.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A standard Text field is capped at 255 characters, which cannot accommodate the expected 3,000-character notes, while Text Area (Long) supports up to 131,072 characters — comfortably meeting the requirement and making it the correct field type choice. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The two field types have significantly different character limits (255 vs. 131,072), making this a meaningful, not purely aesthetic, decision.  \n* B: Standard Text fields are explicitly capped at 255 characters; this is a well-documented, hard field-length limit, not an unlimited-length option.  \n* C: Text Area (Long) fields are fully accessible and usable in Apex like any other text-based field type; there is no such restriction preventing their use in code."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "Choose 2. A developer is reviewing a data model where a custom object \"Shipment__c\" has a master-detail relationship to \"Order__c\". Which two consequences of this master-detail configuration are accurate?", options: [("A", "Shipment__c inherits its sharing and security settings from Order__c rather than having independently configurable Organization-Wide Defaults."), ("B", "Deleting the parent Order__c record automatically cascades and deletes all related Shipment__c child records."), ("C", "Shipment__c records can be freely reparented to a different Order__c at any time with no special configuration required."), ("D", "The Order__c lookup field on Shipment__c can be freely left blank, since master-detail relationships make the parent field optional by default."), ("E", "Roll-up summary fields cannot be created on Order__c to aggregate values from Shipment__c under any circumstances.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Master-detail relationships cascade-delete children when the parent is deleted, and the child object inherits its sharing/security model from the parent rather than having its own independent OWD — both are defining, accurate characteristics of master-detail relationships. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Master-detail relationships make the parent field required, not optional — a child record cannot exist without a parent, which is the opposite of what this option claims.  \n* D: Master-detail child records are locked from reparenting by default; reparenting requires explicitly enabling it in Setup, so it is not freely available with \"no special configuration.\"  \n* E: Roll-up summary fields absolutely can be created on Order__c to aggregate Shipment__c values — this is one of the defining capabilities that master-detail relationships (unlike lookups) specifically enable."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "A developer needs to build a feature that lets a support agent generate a draft response to a customer email, incorporating relevant Knowledge articles, but wants a human to review and edit the draft before sending. Which Salesforce capability is best aligned with this use case?", options: [("A", "A Roll-Up Summary field that automatically concatenates matching Knowledge article text into the response."), ("B", "A validation rule that blocks email sending until an agent manually types a response with no assistance."), ("C", "A hardcoded Apex string template with no AI involvement, since generative drafting is outside programmatic capability entirely."), ("D", "Agentforce, using a prompt template that grounds the AI response in relevant org data (like Knowledge articles), producing a generative draft suited for human review — appropriate because this is a generative, non-transactional task rather than one requiring guaranteed deterministic output.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: This is a generative, non-transactional task (drafting a response for human review, not making a deterministic decision), which is exactly the kind of use case Agentforce with grounded prompt templates is designed for — producing a helpful draft while keeping a human in the loop for final review. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields aggregate numeric values from master-detail child records; they have no capability to search, select, or synthesize relevant Knowledge article text into a coherent draft response.  \n* B: This describes providing no automation assistance at all, which doesn't address the stated goal of generating a helpful draft to speed up and improve the agent's response process.  \n* C: While technically possible, a rigid hardcoded template cannot dynamically incorporate relevant Knowledge content or adapt to the specifics of each customer email the way a grounded generative approach can."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "Choose 2. A developer is deciding how to expose object and field metadata dynamically for a generic Apex utility that must work across many custom objects without hardcoding. Which two Schema-namespace capabilities are relevant and accurate?", options: [("A", "Schema.getGlobalDescribe() returns a Map<String, Schema.SObjectType> covering every object (standard and custom) visible to the running context."), ("B", "DescribeFieldResult exposes metadata such as field label, data type, length, and picklist values (via getPicklistValues() for picklist fields)."), ("C", "Schema.getGlobalDescribe() only returns standard objects and can never include custom objects."), ("D", "DescribeFieldResult can be used to perform DML operations directly on the described field."), ("E", "DescribeSObjectResult can be used to check object-level CRUD permissions like isCreateable() and isUpdateable() for the running user.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Schema.getGlobalDescribe() does cover all objects — standard and custom — visible to the current context, and DescribeFieldResult does expose rich field metadata including label, type, length, and picklist values — both are accurate, foundational facts about the Schema namespace. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: This is factually incorrect — getGlobalDescribe() includes both standard and custom objects, not standard objects exclusively.  \n* D: DescribeFieldResult is purely metadata inspection (label, type, length, accessibility); it has no capability to perform DML operations on any field or record.  \n* E: While DescribeSObjectResult does expose some object-level information, the specific isCreateable()/isUpdateable() checks are actually surfaced through Schema.SObjectType directly (e.g., Schema.SObjectType.Account.isCreateable()) rather than being framed as a DescribeSObjectResult-specific capability in the way this option implies, making it a less precise pairing than A and B."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "A developer must decide the field type for a \"Contract Expiration\" attribute where only the calendar day matters (no time-of-day precision needed), versus a \"Digital Signature Timestamp\" attribute needed for minute-level SLA calculations. What is the correct field type choice for each, and why?", options: [("A", "\"Contract Expiration\" should be a Date field (calendar day only, no time precision needed), while \"Digital Signature Timestamp\" should be a Date/Time field, since it needs to preserve both date and time (down to the minute) for SLA calculations."), ("B", "Both should be Date/Time fields, since Date/Time fields can always be truncated to show only the date portion with zero downside."), ("C", "Both should be Date fields, since Salesforce is deprecating Date/Time fields in favor of Date fields for all new development."), ("D", "Both should be Currency fields, since Currency fields support the highest numeric precision for any calculation, including date-based SLA math.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Choosing the field type based on actual precision need is correct practice — Date is appropriate when only the calendar day matters, while Date/Time is required when time-of-day precision (like minute-level SLA tracking) is genuinely needed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: While Date/Time fields can be formatted for display, they carry more overhead and imply time precision that isn't relevant for a simple calendar-day attribute like Contract Expiration; using the right-sized type for each field is still the better and expected practice.  \n* C: Salesforce is not deprecating Date/Time fields; both Date and Date/Time remain fully supported, distinct field types serving different precision needs.  \n* D: Currency is a monetary data type meant for financial amounts; it is fundamentally inappropriate for storing or calculating date or timestamp values."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "Choose 2. A developer is finalizing a data model for a custom object, \"Case_Escalation__c\", and needs it to support a Roll-Up Summary field on its parent \"Case\" object counting the number of high-severity Case_Escalation__c records, filtered by a Severity picklist equal to \"Critical\". Which two facts about this configuration are accurate?", options: [("A", "A lookup relationship from Case_Escalation__c to Case would work equally well for this roll-up summary configuration."), ("B", "COUNT-type roll-up summary fields cannot use filter criteria; only SUM, MIN, and MAX support filtering."), ("C", "The roll-up summary field will recalculate only once every 24 hours via a scheduled background job."), ("D", "The relationship from Case_Escalation__c to Case must be master-detail (not lookup) for the roll-up summary field to be configurable."), ("E", "The roll-up summary field can use the COUNT aggregate type with filter criteria specifying Severity__c equals \"Critical\", so only matching child records are counted.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "Why correct: The relationship must be master-detail for any roll-up summary field to be configurable at all, and COUNT is a fully supported aggregate type that can be filtered by criteria (like Severity__c equals \"Critical\") so only matching child records are tallied — both statements accurately describe this configuration. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A lookup relationship does not support native roll-up summary fields at all; only master-detail relationships enable this declarative aggregation capability.  \n* B: COUNT-type roll-up summary fields absolutely do support filter criteria, just like SUM, MIN, and MAX; filtering is available across all supported aggregate types, not restricted to only three of them.  \n* C: Roll-up summary fields recalculate automatically and immediately whenever a contributing child record changes — there is no 24-hour batch delay or scheduled job involved.\n\n# Process Automation and Logic"),
        Question(id: "28", question: "A developer needs to build a business process that both fires automatically whenever a record's Status changes to \"Escalated\" AND allows a rep to manually trigger the same escalation logic from a button, sharing the same underlying logic. Which Flow types and design pattern correctly satisfy this without duplicating logic?", options: [("A", "A Record-Triggered Flow for the automatic case and a completely separate, independently written Screen Flow for the manual case, with no shared logic between them."), ("B", "Only Apex triggers support being invoked both automatically and manually; Flow cannot support this pattern at all."), ("C", "A Scheduled-Triggered Flow, since scheduled flows can be both time-based and manually invoked from a button with no additional configuration."), ("D", "A single Autolaunched Flow (or Apex-invocable subflow) containing the core escalation logic, called both by a Record-Triggered Flow (for the automatic case) and by a Screen Flow launched from a button (for the manual case), avoiding duplicated logic.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Building the core logic once in a reusable autolaunched subflow, then calling it from both a Record-Triggered Flow (automatic path) and a Screen Flow (manual, button-launched path), avoids duplicating logic while satisfying both trigger mechanisms. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Writing two independent, non-shared implementations duplicates logic, creating a maintenance burden where a future change must be made in two places and risks the two paths drifting out of sync.  \n* B: Flow fully supports this reusable-subflow pattern; Apex is not the only technology capable of sharing logic across automatic and manual invocation paths.  \n* C: Scheduled-Triggered Flows run on a recurring cron schedule against batches of matching records; they are not designed to also be manually invoked from a button click."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "Choose 2. A developer is comparing before-save and after-save Record-Triggered Flow contexts for a new feature. Which two statements accurately describe capability differences between them?", options: [("A", "After-save flows can perform DML on related records, send emails, and invoke Apex, but consume additional transaction resources compared to before-save updates."), ("B", "Before-save flows can perform DML on unrelated objects and make external callouts, identical to after-save flows."), ("C", "Before-save flows execute after the record has already been committed to the database."), ("D", "After-save flows cannot invoke Apex actions under any circumstances."), ("E", "Before-save flows can update fields on the triggering record directly without requiring separate DML, since the update is folded into the same save operation.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "Why correct: Before-save flows can efficiently update the triggering record's own fields as part of the same save with no extra DML, while after-save flows can perform DML on related records, send emails, and invoke Apex — but this additional capability comes at the cost of extra transaction overhead compared to a fast before-save update. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Before-save flows explicitly cannot make callouts and cannot perform DML on unrelated objects; these capabilities are reserved for after-save (and specifically asynchronous) contexts.  \n* C: This describes after-save flow timing, not before-save — before-save flows run before the record is committed, which is the opposite of what this option claims.  \n* D: After-save flows absolutely can invoke Apex actions (via invocable Apex methods); this is one of their defining, commonly used capabilities."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "A record-triggered Flow updates the same Opportunity record it was triggered from, and this update re-triggers the same Flow again since it runs on both create and update. What Salesforce platform safeguard exists as a backstop against this specific recursion risk, and what should the developer also do?", options: [("A", "Salesforce enforces a recursion depth safeguard (a savepoint/rollback mechanism capping repeated same-record update cycles within a transaction) to prevent an infinite loop from crashing the transaction; the developer should still add explicit recursion-control logic (e.g., a flag field or entry condition) rather than relying solely on the platform backstop."), ("B", "Salesforce has no recursion protection whatsoever for record-triggered Flows; an infinite loop will run until the org's daily API limit is exhausted."), ("C", "Recursion protection only applies to Apex triggers, never to Flow, making this scenario impossible to safeguard declaratively."), ("D", "The Flow will automatically detect and skip any update that would re-trigger itself, requiring no developer action at all.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce does enforce a documented recursion safeguard that limits repeated same-record update cycles within a single transaction as a backstop against runaway loops, but best practice is still to add explicit recursion-control logic (a flag or entry condition check) rather than relying on the platform limit as the primary defense, since hitting that limit itself throws an error. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This is inaccurate — Salesforce does have a built-in recursion depth safeguard specifically to prevent an infinite Flow update loop from running unchecked.  \n* C: Recursion safeguards are not exclusive to Apex; record-triggered Flow has its own documented recursion protections precisely because Flow-driven update loops are a known and common risk.  \n* D: There is no automatic, zero-configuration detection that skips a self-triggering update; explicit recursion-control design (flags, entry criteria) is the developer's responsibility."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "Choose 2. A developer is deciding between an @InvocableMethod design where the input parameter is a List<CustomWrapper> versus one where it mistakenly accepts a single CustomWrapper (not wrapped in a List). Which two facts explain why the List-based signature is required?", options: [("A", "Salesforce Flow always passes a List to an invocable method, even when only a single record is being processed, so a non-List parameter type causes the method signature to be incompatible and unusable from Flow."), ("B", "Using a List parameter allows the invocable method to be properly bulkified, correctly handling scenarios where Flow (e.g., inside a loop, or triggered for multiple records) invokes the method with multiple entries in a single call."), ("C", "A single CustomWrapper parameter (not a List) is actually the required and only valid signature for @InvocableMethod; List parameters are unsupported."), ("D", "The List requirement only applies to methods called from Process Builder, not from Flow."), ("E", "Invocable methods are restricted to primitive parameter types only, so any wrapper class parameter (List or not) is invalid regardless of bulkification concerns.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: The @InvocableMethod signature strictly requires a single List parameter because Salesforce Flow always passes inputs as a List (even for one record), and this design enables proper bulkification so the method can correctly process multiple entries in a single invocation rather than being called repeatedly per record. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: This is the opposite of the actual requirement — a single non-List wrapper parameter is NOT a valid @InvocableMethod signature; a List parameter is mandatory.  \n* D: The List parameter requirement applies universally to @InvocableMethod regardless of caller (Flow, Process Builder, or Agentforce) — it is not caller-specific.  \n* E: Invocable methods commonly accept wrapper class parameters (via @InvocableVariable-decorated properties), not just primitives; the List requirement, not a primitive-only restriction, is the actual signature rule."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "A developer needs to write Apex that safely narrows a Decimal value to an Integer, but is uncertain whether implicit widening or explicit casting applies in the reverse direction. Which statement correctly describes the type conversion behavior?", options: [("A", "Implicit widening automatically converts a narrower type (like Integer) to a wider type (like Decimal or Double) when assigned; converting the reverse direction (wide to narrow, e.g., Decimal to Integer) requires explicit casting and risks losing precision."), ("B", "Both directions of conversion (Integer to Decimal and Decimal to Integer) happen implicitly with zero syntax required and with no risk of data loss in either direction."), ("C", "Apex does not support any numeric type conversion between Integer and Decimal under any circumstances."), ("D", "Explicit casting is required only when converting Boolean to String, never for numeric type conversions.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Implicit widening automatically handles narrow-to-wide numeric conversions (Integer to Decimal) with no special syntax, while the reverse (wide to narrow, like Decimal to Integer) requires an explicit cast using parentheses, and this narrowing risks losing the decimal precision. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Converting Decimal to Integer is not implicit and does carry a real risk of losing the fractional/decimal portion of the value — this direction absolutely requires explicit casting.  \n* C: Apex fully supports numeric type conversion between Integer and Decimal; this claim of zero support is factually incorrect.  \n* D: Explicit casting is required for numeric narrowing conversions (like Decimal to Integer) as well, not exclusively for Boolean-to-String conversions."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to decide between using a List, a Set, and a Map for a piece of Apex logic that must check membership of thousands of Account Ids repeatedly inside a loop, and separately needs to preserve insertion order for a different piece of logic. Which two statements are accurate?", options: [("A", "A Set's contains() method offers fast, near-constant-time membership checks, making it the right choice for repeated Id membership testing inside a loop."), ("B", "A List preserves insertion order and allows duplicate values, making it appropriate when order matters and duplicates are acceptable."), ("C", "A Map cannot use an Id as its key type; only String keys are supported."), ("D", "A Set allows duplicate values by design, identical to a List in this respect."), ("E", "A List's contains() method offers the same near-constant-time performance as a Set's contains() method for large collections.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A Set's contains() check is fast (near O(1)) and ideal for repeated membership testing against many Ids, while a List correctly preserves insertion order and permits duplicates — both statements accurately reflect the appropriate use case for each collection type. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Map keys in Apex can be of virtually any type including Id, String, Integer, or sObject; restricting keys to String only is incorrect.  \n* D: A Set specifically disallows duplicate values by definition (enforcing uniqueness); this is the opposite of a List's behavior, which explicitly does allow duplicates.  \n* E: A List's contains() method performs a linear (O(n)) scan through the list, which is significantly slower than a Set's near-constant-time lookup for large collections — they do not offer equivalent performance."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "A developer writes: for (Account a : Trigger.new) { List<Contact> cons = [SELECT Id FROM Contact WHERE AccountId = :a.Id]; } inside an Account trigger processing 200 records. What is wrong with this code, and what is the correct fix?", options: [("A", "Nothing is wrong; Salesforce automatically merges all queries inside a loop into a single query behind the scenes at runtime."), ("B", "This performs one SOQL query per Account inside the loop, risking exceeding the 100-query synchronous governor limit with 200 records; the fix is to collect all Account Ids into a Set before the loop, issue a single query with WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds, and use a Map keyed by AccountId (or group results) to look up each Account's related Contacts during the loop."), ("C", "The fix is to convert the Account trigger into a Scheduled-Triggered Flow, which is immune to this issue entirely."), ("D", "The fix is to reduce the trigger's batch size to 50 records so the loop only runs 50 times instead of 200.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: This is the classic \"SOQL inside a loop\" anti-pattern; with 200 Account records the code would issue 200 separate queries, risking the 100-query synchronous limit — the correct fix bulkifies the pattern by querying once outside the loop with an IN clause and organizing results (e.g., in a Map) for fast lookup during iteration. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Salesforce does not automatically merge or optimize queries placed inside a loop; each iteration executes as its own separate, fully-counted SOQL query.  \n* C: Switching to a Scheduled-Triggered Flow does not resolve a fundamentally poor query design; the same SOQL-in-a-loop risk would exist in equivalent Flow logic (e.g., Get Records inside a Loop element) if not bulkified.  \n* D: Salesforce trigger batch sizes are managed by the platform (up to 200 records per invocation) and are not something a developer can arbitrarily reduce to \"fix\" a bulkification problem; the real fix is bulkifying the code itself."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to decide the correct trigger context variables to use for an Account after-update trigger that must detect when the Industry field changed from any value to \"Technology\", and take action only on records where that specific transition occurred. Which two facts about trigger context variables are relevant and accurate?", options: [("A", "Trigger.newMap and Trigger.oldMap are both available on update, providing Id-keyed access to the post-change and pre-change versions of each record respectively."), ("B", "Comparing Trigger.oldMap.get(acc.Id).Industry to acc.Industry (from Trigger.new) for each record correctly identifies which specific records transitioned into the \"Technology\" value."), ("C", "Trigger.old is unavailable on update triggers; only Trigger.new can be accessed in that context."), ("D", "Trigger.newMap is unavailable on insert triggers, since newly inserted records have no prior state."), ("E", "Comparing Trigger.new and Trigger.old requires converting both to Maps manually, since Trigger.oldMap and Trigger.newMap do not exist as built-in context variables.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Trigger.newMap and Trigger.oldMap are both natively available on update (each providing Id-keyed access to the new and old record states respectively), and comparing the old Industry value to the new Industry value per record Id correctly isolates exactly which records made the specific \"changed to Technology\" transition. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Trigger.old is fully available on update triggers (and on delete); this option's claim that it's unavailable on update contradicts documented, standard trigger context behavior.  \n* D: Trigger.newMap IS available on insert (providing Id-keyed access to the newly created records); it is Trigger.oldMap and Trigger.old that are unavailable on insert, not Trigger.newMap.  \n* E: Trigger.oldMap and Trigger.newMap are both built-in, native trigger context variables provided automatically by the platform; no manual Map conversion is required to use them."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "A developer is designing a trigger architecture for an org with five different objects, each needing multiple types of automation logic. What best practice should guide the trigger structure, and why?", options: [("A", "Create as many triggers as needed per object, since Salesforce guarantees a deterministic, developer-controllable execution order across multiple triggers on the same object."), ("B", "Use exactly one trigger per object that delegates to a dedicated handler class with context-specific methods (e.g., onBeforeInsert, onAfterUpdate), since Salesforce does not guarantee execution order when multiple triggers exist on the same object, and this pattern also improves testability by isolating logic in a class independent of the trigger context."), ("C", "Combine all five objects' logic into a single, monolithic trigger defined on the Account object, since Account triggers can intercept DML on any object in the org."), ("D", "Avoid trigger handler classes entirely and always write all logic directly inline in the trigger body for maximum performance.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: One trigger per object routing to a dedicated handler class is the standard best practice because Salesforce does not guarantee execution order across multiple triggers on the same object, and separating logic into a handler class makes it independently unit-testable and easier to maintain. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This is factually incorrect — Salesforce explicitly does NOT guarantee a deterministic order when multiple triggers exist on the same object, which is exactly the risk the one-trigger-per-object pattern avoids.  \n* C: A trigger defined on Account only fires for DML on Account records; it has no ability to intercept or process DML happening on entirely unrelated objects.  \n* D: Writing all logic directly inline in the trigger body makes the logic difficult to unit-test independently and is explicitly discouraged in favor of the handler class pattern; there's also no meaningful \"performance\" benefit to inline logic over a well-structured handler class."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A developer needs to declare a set of fixed status values (NEW, IN_PROGRESS, COMPLETE, CANCELLED) as named constants used throughout multiple Apex classes, with type-safe comparisons and switch statement support. What is the best construct?", options: [("A", "A List<String> containing the four string values, compared using List.contains() everywhere they're needed."), ("B", "An Enum, e.g., public enum RequestStatus { NEW, IN_PROGRESS, COMPLETE, CANCELLED }, referenced as RequestStatus.NEW and usable directly in switch on statements for clean, type-safe branching."), ("C", "Four separate Boolean variables, one for each possible status, manually toggled based on context."), ("D", "A single Text field on a Custom Metadata Type record, queried fresh via SOQL every time a status check is needed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: An Enum is specifically designed for representing a fixed set of named constants with type-safe comparison and direct support in switch statements, making it the cleanest, most idiomatic Apex construct for this exact requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A List<String> of magic strings sacrifices type safety (typos in string literals won't be caught at compile time) and doesn't support the direct, clean switch-statement branching that an Enum provides.  \n* C: Four separate Boolean flags is an unwieldy, error-prone pattern that doesn't scale, doesn't prevent invalid combinations (e.g., two flags true simultaneously), and offers none of an Enum's clarity or safety.  \n* D: Querying a Custom Metadata Type record via SOQL every time a status check is needed is unnecessarily expensive (consuming query governor limits) for what should be a simple, static, compile-time constant set."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "Choose 2. A developer is writing a method that must safely handle a null Account reference without throwing a NullPointerException, and separately needs to understand default values for uninitialized primitive types. Which two statements are accurate?", options: [("A", "All object references (including sObjects and collections) default to null if not explicitly initialized, and accessing a field or method on a null reference throws a NullPointerException at runtime."), ("B", "Primitive types like Integer, Boolean, and Decimal default to 0, false, and 0.0 respectively when uninitialized, never to null."), ("C", "Guarding with a null check (e.g., if (acc != null && acc.Name != null)) before dereferencing is the correct defensive pattern to avoid a NullPointerException."), ("D", "NullPointerException can always be caught and safely ignored without any consequence to program correctness."), ("E", "Integer, Boolean, and Decimal variables default to null, not to 0, false, or 0.0, when uninitialized.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: Uninitialized object references (including sObjects) default to null and dereferencing them throws a NullPointerException, and the correct defensive pattern is exactly the described null-check-before-dereference approach — both statements are accurate and standard Apex behavior. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This is incorrect — in Apex, primitive types like Integer, Boolean, and Decimal actually default to null, not to 0, false, or 0.0, which is a commonly tested and counter-intuitive Apex behavior.  \n* D: While a NullPointerException can be caught in a try-catch block, blindly ignoring it without addressing the underlying null reference risks silently skipping logic and producing incorrect program behavior — it should never be casually \"ignored without consequence.\"  \n* E: This restates option B's incorrect claim inversely as if it were the correct fact, but frames it in a way that omits the accurate detail that Apex primitives specifically default to null — while directionally closer to correct than B, A and C together form the clearest, most complete accurate pairing for this question."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "A developer needs an abstract base class, PaymentProcessor, that defines a method process() which every subclass (CreditCardProcessor, ACHProcessor) must implement differently, while also providing a shared, concrete logValidation() method that all subclasses inherit as-is. What Apex construct is correct?", options: [("A", "An interface, since interfaces can contain both abstract and concrete method implementations mixed together."), ("B", "An abstract class using the abstract keyword, with process() declared abstract (no body, forcing each subclass to implement it) and logValidation() implemented as a normal concrete method that subclasses inherit directly."), ("C", "A Trigger, since triggers are the only Apex construct that supports method overriding."), ("D", "A Custom Metadata Type, since metadata types can define both abstract and concrete method bodies natively.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: An abstract class is specifically designed to mix abstract methods (forcing subclasses to provide their own implementation, like process()) with concrete methods that are inherited as-is (like logValidation()) — exactly matching this requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Interfaces in Apex declare only method signatures with no bodies at all; they cannot contain concrete, already-implemented methods like logValidation() the way an abstract class can.  \n* C: Triggers are not object-oriented class constructs that support inheritance or method overriding at all; this option confuses fundamentally different Apex concepts.  \n* D: Custom Metadata Types are declarative data records with fields, not executable code constructs; they cannot define or implement any methods, abstract or concrete."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "Choose 2. A developer is writing a batch data-cleanup routine and must choose between synchronous Apex and asynchronous Apex (Queueable) based on governor limit needs. Which two facts about synchronous versus asynchronous governor limits are accurate?", options: [("A", "Synchronous Apex transactions are capped at 100 SOQL queries and 6 MB heap size, while asynchronous transactions allow 200 SOQL queries and 12 MB heap size."), ("B", "CPU time is capped at 10,000 ms for synchronous Apex and 60,000 ms for asynchronous Apex, giving async contexts significantly more processing budget."), ("C", "DML statement limits differ between synchronous and asynchronous Apex, with synchronous allowing more DML statements than asynchronous."), ("D", "Synchronous and asynchronous Apex share an identical SOQL query limit of 100 in both contexts."), ("E", "Heap size limits are identical between synchronous and asynchronous Apex at 6 MB each.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Synchronous Apex is capped at 100 SOQL queries and 6 MB heap, while asynchronous Apex allows 200 SOQL queries and 12 MB heap, and CPU time is 10,000 ms synchronous versus 60,000 ms asynchronous — both statements accurately reflect the higher resource ceilings given to asynchronous execution contexts. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: The DML statement limit is actually the same (150) in both synchronous and asynchronous contexts — it does not differ between the two, unlike SOQL, heap, and CPU time limits.  \n* D: This is incorrect — the SOQL query limit differs between the two contexts (100 synchronous vs. 200 asynchronous), it is not identical.  \n* E: This is incorrect — heap size differs (6 MB synchronous vs. 12 MB asynchronous), it is not identical between the two contexts."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "A developer needs a method that can be called from Flow to check inventory availability, returning a Boolean per input record, and the method also needs to be callable from an LWC component using @wire since the check is read-only with no DML. What annotation strategy is correct?", options: [("A", "Apply @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) alone; Flow will automatically recognize and invoke any cacheable Apex method without needing @InvocableMethod."), ("B", "No annotation is needed for either integration surface; Salesforce automatically exposes all public static methods to both Flow and LWC."), ("C", "Apply @InvocableMethod alone; this single annotation automatically also exposes the method for @wire usage from LWC with no additional annotation needed."), ("D", "Since a single Apex method typically serves one integration surface cleanly, create two separate methods: one annotated @InvocableMethod (List-based signature) for Flow, and a separate method annotated @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) for the read-only LWC @wire use case, since @wire requires cacheable=true and no DML.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: @InvocableMethod and @AuraEnabled serve distinct integration surfaces (Flow versus LWC/Aura respectively) with different signature requirements (List-based for invocable, cacheable=true for @wire-compatible read-only methods), so the correct approach is typically two purpose-built methods rather than expecting one annotation to cover both surfaces. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) exposes a method to LWC/Aura only; Flow does not recognize or invoke @AuraEnabled methods — it specifically requires @InvocableMethod.  \n* B: Apex methods are not automatically exposed to Flow or LWC without the correct annotations; both @InvocableMethod and @AuraEnabled are required, explicit opt-ins for their respective integration surfaces.  \n* C: @InvocableMethod exposes a method to Flow (and Agentforce) only; it does not automatically make that method available for @wire usage in LWC, which specifically requires @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true)."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "A developer must write a SOQL query returning the single most recently created Case for a given Contact where Status equals 'Open', but is unsure how to avoid a QueryException if zero or multiple results could match. What approach is correct?", options: [("A", "Use a Roll-Up Summary field to determine the most recent Case's Id, since roll-up summary fields can return a specific child record's Id."), ("B", "Wrap the query in a try-catch block and assume any resulting exception means there are no matching Cases, then default to a hardcoded Id."), ("C", "Assign the query to a List<Case> variable with ORDER BY CreatedDate DESC LIMIT 1, then check if the list is non-empty (list.size() > 0 or !list.isEmpty()) before accessing index 0, safely handling the zero-result case."), ("D", "Assign the query directly to a single Case variable: Case c = [SELECT Id FROM Case WHERE ContactId = :contactId AND Status = 'Open' ORDER BY CreatedDate DESC LIMIT 1]; and rely on this always succeeding regardless of result count.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Assigning to a List (even when expecting at most one result) followed by an explicit empty-check before accessing index 0 is the correct, safe pattern that avoids a QueryException entirely, since a single-sObject assignment throws when the result count isn't exactly one. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Roll-Up Summary fields only support aggregate functions (COUNT, SUM, MIN, MAX) on numeric/date fields; they cannot return a specific child record's Id value.  \n* B: Relying on catching an exception to infer \"no results\" is a fragile anti-pattern; it conflates all possible exception causes into one assumption and doesn't represent proper defensive query handling.  \n* D: Assigning a query directly to a single sObject variable throws a QueryException if the result set has zero rows or more than one row; even with LIMIT 1, a zero-result scenario (no open Cases for the Contact) would throw \"List has no rows for assignment.\""),
        Question(id: "43", question: "Choose 2. A developer is writing a dynamic SOQL query where an object name and a user-supplied filter value are both determined at runtime. Which two practices correctly mitigate SOQL injection risk in this scenario?", options: [("A", "Wrap any user-supplied string used in the dynamic query with String.escapeSingleQuotes() before concatenating it into the query string, escaping single quotes so they cannot be interpreted as SOQL syntax."), ("B", "SOQL injection is not a real risk in Apex; only traditional SQL databases are vulnerable to injection attacks."), ("C", "Avoid Database.query(String) entirely in all cases and always use only static, hardcoded inline SOQL regardless of the requirement for dynamic object/field names."), ("D", "Concatenate user input directly into the dynamic query string with no escaping, since Database.query() automatically sanitizes all inputs internally."), ("E", "Use bind variables (the colon-prefixed syntax) wherever possible instead of raw string concatenation, since bind variables are resolved safely at query parse time.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "Why correct: Escaping user-supplied strings with String.escapeSingleQuotes() and preferring bind variables over raw concatenation are both standard, documented mitigations against SOQL injection when building dynamic queries with runtime-determined values. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: SOQL injection is a real, well-documented risk specific to Apex's dynamic SOQL capability; the general SQL injection concept fully applies here despite SOQL not being traditional SQL.  \n* C: When a genuine requirement exists for dynamic object or field names determined at runtime, Database.query(String) is the only mechanism that supports this; avoiding it entirely isn't a practical option in that scenario — proper sanitization is the correct response instead.  \n* D: Database.query() does not automatically sanitize concatenated input; unescaped user input concatenated directly into a dynamic query string is exactly the vulnerability that leads to SOQL injection."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "A developer's Apex trigger performs an update DML on the same records currently being processed in an after-update trigger context, and needs to understand how this interacts with the save order of execution and potential re-entrancy. What is accurate about this scenario?", options: [("A", "Performing DML inside an after-update trigger on the same records being processed can cause the trigger to fire again for that same update, and this re-entrant execution shares the same transaction and its governor limit counters with the original invocation."), ("B", "Re-triggering from an after-update trigger's DML has no risk of infinite recursion since Salesforce silently discards any DML that would cause a trigger to re-fire."), ("C", "DML performed inside an after-update trigger always executes in a brand new, separate transaction with its own independent governor limits."), ("D", "Triggers can never perform DML on the records currently being processed within their own context; this is blocked by the platform entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: An after-update trigger performing DML on its own triggering records can cause that trigger to fire again, and this re-entrant execution remains within the same overall transaction, meaning all governor limit counters (SOQL, DML, CPU) continue accumulating from the original invocation rather than resetting. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Salesforce does not silently discard such DML; instead, without proper recursion-control logic, this scenario risks genuine infinite recursion, mitigated only by the platform's recursion depth safeguard as a backstop, not automatic silent discarding.  \n* C: DML performed within a trigger's execution stays within the same Apex transaction; it does not spawn a separate transaction with independent governor limits.  \n* D: Triggers absolutely can perform DML on records within their own context (this is a common pattern, e.g., updating a different field on the same records); it is not blocked by the platform."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to implement error handling for a service method that validates loan applications, where specific business rule violations (e.g., \"credit score too low\") should be distinguishable from generic system errors by calling code. Which two practices are correct?", options: [("A", "Create a custom exception class extending Exception (e.g., public class LoanValidationException extends Exception {}), throw it for specific business rule violations, and catch it specifically in calling code to distinguish it from generic Exception types."), ("B", "Since Apex catch blocks execute in a random order at runtime, the order in which catch blocks are written has no effect on which one handles a given exception."), ("C", "Order catch blocks from most specific exception type to most generic, so a specific custom exception is caught by its dedicated catch block rather than falling through to a generic catch (Exception e) block."), ("D", "Custom exceptions must always extend DmlException specifically, never the base Exception class."), ("E", "Use only the generic System Exception type for all error conditions, since custom exception classes are not supported in Apex.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: Extending the base Exception class to create a custom, named exception type lets calling code distinguish specific business rule violations from generic errors, and ordering catch blocks from most specific to most generic ensures the intended, specific handler actually catches the custom exception rather than being masked by a broader catch. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Catch blocks execute in the exact order they are written, from top to bottom, evaluated against the actual exception type thrown — order is deterministic and meaningful, not random.  \n* D: Custom exceptions can extend the base Exception class directly; there is no requirement to extend DmlException specifically unless you specifically want DML-related exception behavior.  \n* E: Custom exception classes are fully supported in Apex via the extends Exception syntax; this is a standard, well-documented capability, not an unsupported one."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "A developer's @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) method, intended to be called via @wire from LWC, attempts to perform an update DML operation inside its body. What happens, and what is the correct fix?", options: [("A", "cacheable=true only restricts DML on custom objects, never on standard objects like Account or Contact."), ("B", "The method throws a runtime exception because cacheable=true methods are strictly read-only and cannot perform DML; the fix is to split the logic into a separate, non-cacheable @AuraEnabled method for the DML operation, called imperatively rather than via @wire."), ("C", "The DML silently succeeds but is automatically rolled back at the end of the transaction with no error surfaced."), ("D", "The method executes normally with no restriction; cacheable=true has no effect on DML capability.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Methods marked @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) are strictly read-only by design (enabling client-side caching and @wire compatibility), so any DML attempt inside them throws a runtime exception; the correct fix is separating read (cacheable) and write (non-cacheable, imperatively called) concerns into distinct methods. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The read-only restriction on cacheable=true methods applies universally to DML on any object, standard or custom — there is no object-type-based exception to this rule.  \n* C: The DML does not silently succeed and roll back; it throws an explicit exception at the DML statement itself, immediately surfacing the error rather than silently failing.  \n* D: cacheable=true absolutely does restrict DML — this is one of its most important and commonly tested constraints, not a no-op setting."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to expose an Apex method as a Flow action that accepts a Contact record Id and a discount percentage, then returns a calculated final price as output. Which two implementation details are required for a correctly structured @InvocableMethod?", options: [("A", "The method must return a single, non-List instance of the output wrapper class directly, since returning a List would confuse Flow Builder's output mapping."), ("B", "The output wrapper class does not need any special annotation, since Flow automatically detects public properties as outputs without any decoration."), ("C", "The method must be annotated with @AuraEnabled instead of @InvocableMethod, since @InvocableMethod is deprecated in favor of @AuraEnabled for all new development."), ("D", "The input wrapper class properties (Contact Id and discount percentage) must be annotated with @InvocableVariable so Flow Builder recognizes them as configurable input fields."), ("E", "The method signature must accept a List of the input wrapper type and return a List of the output wrapper type (or void), since Flow always passes and expects collections even for single-record invocations.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "Why correct: @InvocableVariable is required on wrapper class properties for Flow Builder to recognize them as configurable inputs/outputs, and the method signature must use List-based input and output types because Flow always passes and expects collections, even for a single record — both are core, accurate requirements for a correctly structured invocable method. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The correct signature returns a List of the output wrapper type (matching the List-based convention), not a single non-List instance; Flow's convention consistently uses Lists for both directions.  \n* B: Output wrapper class properties still require @InvocableVariable annotation to be recognized and mapped correctly by Flow Builder; there is no automatic detection of plain public properties without it.  \n* C: @InvocableMethod is not deprecated and remains the correct, required annotation for exposing Apex methods to Flow; @AuraEnabled serves an entirely different purpose (LWC/Aura exposure)."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "A developer needs to implement a class that will be run on a recurring nightly schedule to archive old records, using System.schedule(). Which interface must the class implement, and what is the required method signature?", options: [("A", "The class must implement Schedulable, with a public void execute(SchedulableContext sc) method, then be scheduled using System.schedule('Job Name', cronExpression, new MyScheduledClass())."), ("B", "The class must implement Database.Batchable<SObject>, since only Batchable classes can be run on a schedule."), ("C", "The class must extend the base Trigger class and override its onSchedule() method."), ("D", "The class must implement Queueable, with a method named run() that takes no parameters.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: A class intended to run on a recurring schedule via System.schedule() must implement the Schedulable interface with the exact required method signature public void execute(SchedulableContext sc), which the platform calls at the scheduled time defined by the cron expression. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Database.Batchable defines start(), execute(), and finish() for processing large record sets in chunks; a Batchable class doesn't run on a schedule by itself — it is commonly invoked FROM within a Schedulable class's execute() method, but Schedulable is still the required scheduling interface.  \n* C: There is no such thing as extending a base Trigger class with an onSchedule() override in Apex; this describes a nonexistent construct.  \n* D: Queueable is used for asynchronous jobs enqueued via System.enqueueJob(), not for time-based recurring scheduling; its required method is execute(QueueableContext ctx), and it has no run() method requirement."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to process 2 million records nightly with complex per-record logic, chunked automatically by the platform, with the ability to preserve a running total across all chunks. Which two Batch Apex design elements are required?", options: [("A", "A List<SObject> passed directly as the start() return type instead of a QueryLocator, since Lists support unlimited record volume without any SOQL row limit."), ("B", "Additionally implementing Database.Stateful alongside Database.Batchable, so instance variables (like a running total) persist their values across every execute() call rather than resetting with each new chunk."), ("C", "Batch Apex chunk size defaults to 2,000 records and cannot be configured to any other value."), ("D", "Implementing Database.Batchable<SObject> with a start() method returning a Database.QueryLocator (supporting up to 50 million records, well beyond the 50,000-record SOQL limit), and execute()/finish() methods to process each chunk and finalize results."), ("E", "Instance variables in a Batchable class persist across execute() calls by default with no additional interface needed.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Why correct: A Database.QueryLocator supports up to 50 million records (bypassing the standard 50,000-record SOQL limit) making it correct for processing 2 million records, and Database.Stateful is specifically required to preserve instance variable values (like a running total) across all execute() calls rather than resetting with each chunk. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A List<SObject> return type from start() is still bound by normal SOQL query limits (50,000 records) since it must be populated via a query first; it does not support unlimited volume the way QueryLocator does.  \n* C: The default batch size is 200 records, not 2,000, and it can be explicitly configured (up to a maximum of 2,000) via the optional scope parameter in Database.executeBatch().  \n* E: This is the opposite of actual behavior — by default, each execute() call gets a fresh instance with reset instance variables; Database.Stateful is specifically required to preserve state across calls."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "A developer's @future(callout=true) method is failing to compile because it accepts an Account sObject as a parameter. What is the issue, and what is the correct fix?", options: [("A", "@future methods can accept any parameter type including sObjects; the compile error must be caused by an unrelated syntax issue."), ("B", "The fix is to mark the Account parameter with @InvocableVariable to make it compatible with @future."), ("C", "@future methods require all parameters to be marked transient to be valid, regardless of type."), ("D", "@future methods cannot accept sObject parameters at all; only primitive types (String, Integer, Boolean, etc.) or collections of primitives are allowed, since the record's state could change between when the method is invoked and when it actually executes asynchronously — the fix is to pass the record's Id (or a Set/List of Ids) instead and re-query fresh data inside the method.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: @future methods have a hard platform restriction disallowing sObject parameters (and non-primitive collections) specifically because the record's state in the database could change between the synchronous call and the later asynchronous execution; the standard fix is passing Ids and re-querying fresh data inside the future method. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: This restriction is a real, well-documented, and strictly enforced compile-time rule for @future methods — it is not an unrelated issue masking a different syntax error.  \n* B: @InvocableVariable is specifically for annotating properties within @InvocableMethod input/output wrapper classes for Flow; it has no relevance to or effect on @future method parameter type restrictions.  \n* C: There is no requirement that @future method parameters be marked transient; the actual restriction is specifically about disallowing sObject types (and non-primitive collections), unrelated to the transient keyword."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to chain multiple asynchronous jobs together, where Job B should only start after Job A fully completes, and wants to pass state or context between them. Which two facts about Queueable Apex chaining are accurate?", options: [("A", "@future methods support the exact same chaining capability as Queueable, since both are asynchronous Apex mechanisms with identical constraints."), ("B", "Chained Queueable jobs execute simultaneously in parallel with the job that enqueued them, never sequentially."), ("C", "From a synchronous context (e.g., a standard UI save or trigger), up to 50 Queueable jobs can be enqueued via System.enqueueJob() in a single transaction; but from within an already-executing asynchronous Queueable job's execute() method, only 1 child Queueable job can be enqueued — this is the limit that governs chaining depth."), ("D", "A Queueable job can enqueue another Queueable job from within its own execute() method, and this chaining is a documented, supported pattern for sequential asynchronous processing."), ("E", "Queueable Apex cannot accept sObject or other complex object parameters in its constructor, only primitives, identical to @future method restrictions.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Queueable Apex explicitly supports chaining — enqueuing a new Queueable job from within an already-running Queueable's execute() method — and the governor limit that actually constrains this is 50 jobs enqueued per synchronous transaction versus only 1 job enqueued per asynchronous transaction, which is what limits how deep a chain of Queueable jobs launched from within other async jobs can go. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: @future methods do not support chaining at all — you cannot call another @future method from within an executing @future method; this is a key differentiator that makes Queueable more powerful for sequential workflows.  \n* B: Chained Queueable jobs execute sequentially, not in parallel — the second job is only enqueued and begins execution after the first job's execute() method completes, which is the entire point of the chaining pattern.  \n* E: Unlike @future methods, Queueable Apex constructors CAN accept complex object parameters including sObjects, since the Queueable instance itself is serialized and its state preserved until execution — this is a major advantage over @future's primitive-only restriction."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "A developer needs to understand precisely where in Salesforce's save order of execution a workflow rule's field update sits relative to validation rules and triggers, since a workflow field update is causing validation rules to seemingly re-run. What is accurate about this behavior?", options: [("A", "Workflow rules execute entirely outside of and unrelated to the documented save order of execution."), ("B", "Workflow field updates never cause any re-evaluation of validation rules or triggers; the described behavior must be caused by an unrelated bug."), ("C", "When a workflow rule's field update fires, Salesforce re-runs the system validation and custom validation rules (along with before/after triggers) against the updated field value, as part of the documented save order — this is why validation appears to \"re-run\" after the workflow field update."), ("D", "Validation rules only ever run once per save operation under all circumstances, with zero exceptions, regardless of what automation fires afterward.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce's documented save order of execution specifies that when a workflow rule's field update occurs, the platform re-runs validation (system and custom) and before/after triggers against the newly updated value — this re-evaluation is expected, documented platform behavior, not a bug. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Workflow rules are explicitly part of the documented save order of execution, occurring at a specific point in that sequence — they do not operate entirely outside or unrelated to it.  \n* B: This directly contradicts the documented save order of execution, which explicitly describes workflow field updates triggering a re-run of validation and trigger logic.  \n* D: Validation rules can indeed be re-evaluated more than once in a single save operation specifically when a workflow field update causes the documented re-run behavior; the \"only ever once, zero exceptions\" claim is inaccurate."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to implement a partial rollback within a single Apex transaction: attempt a risky secondary operation, and if it fails, undo only that operation's changes while preserving earlier, already-successful work in the same transaction. Which two facts about savepoints are accurate?", options: [("A", "Governor limit counts (such as the number of SOQL queries or DML statements already consumed) are restored to their pre-savepoint values when Database.rollback(sp) is called."), ("B", "Savepoint sp = Database.setSavepoint(); captures the transaction's state at that point, and Database.rollback(sp); later undoes all DML performed after that savepoint was set, without rolling back the entire transaction."), ("C", "A savepoint can be used to roll back a portion of a transaction's DML changes while keeping earlier DML changes (made before the savepoint) intact."), ("D", "Database.rollback(sp) only undoes DML on the specific object type that caused the failure, leaving DML on other object types intact even if performed after the savepoint."), ("E", "Only one savepoint can ever be set per transaction; attempting to set a second savepoint throws a runtime exception.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "Why correct: Database.setSavepoint() and Database.rollback(sp) work exactly as described — capturing a point-in-time state and later undoing all DML after that point while preserving earlier, pre-savepoint DML — giving developers precise, partial rollback control within a single transaction. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Governor limit counts (SOQL queries, DML statements, etc.) are NOT restored after a rollback; the resource consumption incurred before the rollback still counts against the transaction's overall limits.  \n* D: Database.rollback(sp) undoes ALL DML performed after the savepoint was set, regardless of which object type it affected — it is not selective by object type.  \n* E: Multiple savepoints can be set within a single transaction (each call to Database.setSavepoint() creates a new one); there is no hard one-savepoint-per-transaction restriction."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "A developer needs a method that logs debug information at the FINEST level only during a specific troubleshooting scenario, without affecting standard DEBUG-level logging used throughout the rest of the org's Apex code. What is the correct approach to writing this log statement?", options: [("A", "FINEST is not a valid logging level in Salesforce; only ERROR, WARN, and INFO are supported."), ("B", "System.debug(LoggingLevel.FINEST, 'Detailed troubleshooting info: ' + someVariable); explicitly specifies the FINEST log level for this specific statement, independent of other System.debug() calls using the default DEBUG level."), ("C", "System.debug() statements always log at the FINEST level automatically; no level specification is possible or necessary."), ("D", "Logging levels can only be set org-wide and cannot be specified on a per-statement basis within Apex code.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: System.debug() accepts an optional LoggingLevel parameter, allowing a specific statement to log at a chosen level (like FINEST) independently of other calls using the default DEBUG level, giving fine-grained control per log statement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: FINEST is indeed a valid, documented Salesforce logging level (part of the full sequence: NONE, ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG, FINE, FINER, FINEST); claiming it doesn't exist is factually incorrect.  \n* C: System.debug() defaults to the DEBUG level when no level is specified; it does not automatically log at FINEST unless explicitly told to do so.  \n* D: Logging level can absolutely be specified per individual System.debug() statement in code, in addition to the trace flag's overall log level configuration in Setup — it is not solely an org-wide setting."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to decide how to automate a business process requiring: simple conditional field updates on save (no DML on other objects), AND a separate requirement needing complex algorithmic logic with tight, governor-limit-sensitive bulk processing across 50,000 records nightly. Which two tool choices correctly match each sub-requirement?", options: [("A", "The simple conditional field-update requirement (no DML on other objects) is well-suited to a before-save Record-Triggered Flow, requiring no code or tests."), ("B", "The nightly bulk processing requirement should use a before-save Record-Triggered Flow instead of Batch Apex, since before-save Flows have no governor limit constraints whatsoever."), ("C", "The complex, high-volume, governor-limit-sensitive nightly bulk processing requirement is well-suited to Batch Apex, which is specifically designed for processing large record volumes in governor-limit-safe chunks."), ("D", "Both requirements should use the same single before-save Flow, since before-save Flows can handle any volume of records with any complexity level."), ("E", "The simple conditional field-update requirement should use Batch Apex exclusively, since Batch Apex is required for any automation touching more than one record.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: A before-save Record-Triggered Flow is the right, code-free tool for simple same-record conditional field updates, while Batch Apex is specifically designed for complex, high-volume, governor-limit-sensitive nightly processing across tens of thousands of records — correctly matching each sub-requirement to its appropriate tool. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Before-save Flows are absolutely subject to governor limits like any other Salesforce automation; claiming no constraints whatsoever is factually incorrect, and they are not designed for large-scale nightly batch processing.  \n* D: Before-save Flows are not suited to complex, high-volume, governor-limit-sensitive bulk processing; that scenario specifically exceeds Flow's practical capabilities and requires Batch Apex.  \n* E: Batch Apex is not \"required\" for any multi-record automation — it is specifically suited to large-volume, chunked processing; a simple conditional field update on a single record's save is far better handled by a lightweight before-save Flow.\n\n# User Interface"),
        Question(id: "56", question: "A developer needs to build an Apex web service method that safely returns Account data to an LWC component, ensuring the running user's field-level security and object permissions are respected before the data leaves the server. Which approach is correct?", options: [("A", "Rely solely on the Visualforce page's escape='true' attribute to enforce field-level security on returned Apex data."), ("B", "Use WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED in the SOQL query (or Security.stripInaccessible(AccessType.READABLE, records)) to enforce field- and object-level security before the data is returned, since with sharing alone only enforces row-level (record) sharing, not FLS."), ("C", "Query the data normally with a plain SOQL statement and return it directly with no additional security check, since @AuraEnabled automatically enforces FLS on every returned field."), ("D", "Declare the class without sharing, since without sharing automatically applies stricter FLS enforcement than with sharing.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: with/without sharing controls only row-level (record) access; field-level security must be enforced separately using WITH SECURITY_ENFORCED in SOQL or Security.stripInaccessible(), which is exactly the correct, documented approach for ensuring FLS compliance before returning data to a client component. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The escape='true' attribute in Visualforce controls HTML output encoding (XSS prevention) for displayed text; it has no relationship to field-level security enforcement on Apex-returned data.  \n* C: @AuraEnabled does not automatically enforce FLS on returned fields; this is a common misconception, and explicit enforcement (SECURITY_ENFORCED or stripInaccessible) is required.  \n* D: without sharing ignores the running user's record-level sharing rules entirely (running in system mode for sharing purposes) and has no relationship to field-level security enforcement, let alone \"stricter\" enforcement."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "Choose 2. A developer is reviewing a class for XSS (cross-site scripting) risk in Visualforce and separately reviewing SOQL injection risk in a related Apex controller. Which two mitigations are correctly matched to their respective vulnerability type?", options: [("A", "Visualforce's {! expression } syntax never encodes output; all encoding must always be added manually with HTMLENCODE() regardless of context."), ("B", "In Visualforce, the {! expression } merge syntax automatically HTML-encodes output by default, converting characters like < and > to prevent script injection; explicit HTMLENCODE() calls are needed for manually constructed strings or specific contexts requiring explicit encoding."), ("C", "HTMLENCODE() is the correct mitigation against SOQL injection, while String.escapeSingleQuotes() is the correct mitigation against XSS."), ("D", "SOQL injection and XSS are the same vulnerability with identical mitigation techniques, since both involve unsanitized user input."), ("E", "In Apex dynamic SOQL, wrapping user-supplied input with String.escapeSingleQuotes() before concatenation is the documented mitigation against SOQL injection.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Why correct: Visualforce's default merge syntax auto-encodes output to guard against XSS (with HTMLENCODE() available for extra explicit cases), while String.escapeSingleQuotes() is the documented, standard mitigation specifically for SOQL injection in dynamic queries — both statements correctly match mitigation to vulnerability type. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Visualforce's {! expression } syntax does auto-encode output by default; claiming it never encodes anything contradicts well-documented, standard Visualforce behavior.  \n* C: This swaps the mitigations backward — HTMLENCODE() addresses XSS (HTML/script injection), while String.escapeSingleQuotes() addresses SOQL injection, not the reverse.  \n* D: XSS (malicious script injection into rendered HTML) and SOQL injection (malicious alteration of query logic) are distinct vulnerability types with different attack vectors and different, non-interchangeable mitigation techniques."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "A developer needs to check, before performing an insert DML in Apex, whether the running user has create permission on the Opportunity object, and separately needs to check field-level access to the Amount field before returning it in a response. What are the correct method calls for each check?", options: [("A", "Security.stripInaccessible(AccessType.CREATABLE, records) is the only valid way to check object-level create permission; Schema-based describe methods cannot check CRUD."), ("B", "Schema.SObjectType.Opportunity.isAccessible() for both checks, since isAccessible() covers both object-level and field-level permissions simultaneously."), ("C", "Schema.SObjectType.Opportunity.isCreateable() for the object-level create check, and Schema.SObjectType.Opportunity.fields.Amount.getDescribe().isAccessible() for the field-level read-access check."), ("D", "FLS and object CRUD checks are automatically enforced by every with sharing class with no explicit method calls required.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: isCreateable() on the SObjectType is the correct, standard method for the object-level create permission check, while calling getDescribe().isAccessible() on the specific field token is the correct method for checking field-level read access — these are two distinct checks at two distinct levels, each with its own dedicated method. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Security.stripInaccessible() is one valid, bulk-oriented approach for filtering records by access, but Schema.SObjectType's isCreateable(), isAccessible(), isUpdateable(), and isDeletable() methods are equally valid, commonly used, and explicitly documented ways to check CRUD permissions.  \n* B: isAccessible() at the object level checks object-level read permission specifically, not create permission, and it does not simultaneously cover field-level checks — field-level checks require calling getDescribe() on the specific field.  \n* D: with sharing only enforces row-level (record) sharing rules; it does not automatically enforce object-level CRUD or field-level security — those require the explicit checks described in this scenario."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "Choose 2. A developer is building a Visualforce page and needs to decide between a Custom Controller and a Controller Extension for a new requirement: display an Account record with all standard save/cancel/edit behavior preserved, plus one additional custom method for a specialized calculation. Which two facts correctly support choosing a Controller Extension here?", options: [("A", "A Custom Controller is required whenever any additional custom method is needed beyond what the Standard Controller natively provides."), ("B", "The extension's constructor receives the Standard Controller as a parameter, allowing the extension to access the record via stdController.getRecord() while still benefiting from standard behavior."), ("C", "Controller Extensions cannot access the underlying record; only Custom Controllers can query and manipulate record data."), ("D", "A Controller Extension augments the Standard Controller (standardController='Account' with extensions='MyExtension'), preserving standard actions like save, cancel, and edit while adding custom methods and properties."), ("E", "Using extensions='MyExtension' completely replaces and disables all standard controller actions, requiring the developer to reimplement save and cancel manually.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Why correct: A Controller Extension is specifically designed to add custom methods/properties while preserving all standard controller behavior (save, cancel, edit), and its constructor receiving the Standard Controller as a parameter (accessed via getRecord()) is exactly the mechanism that enables this augmentation — both facts directly support choosing an extension for this scenario. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: A Custom Controller (fully replacing the Standard Controller) is only needed when standard behavior doesn't fit at all or there's no single-record context; adding one additional custom method while keeping standard actions is exactly the Controller Extension use case, not a reason to require a full custom controller.  \n* C: Controller Extensions absolutely can access the underlying record, specifically via the Standard Controller reference passed into their constructor (stdController.getRecord()); this is a core, defining capability of extensions.  \n* E: Using extensions does not disable standard actions — save, cancel, and edit remain fully functional and available; that preservation is precisely the point of using an extension over a full custom controller."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "A Visualforce page is throwing a view state size error after a developer added several instance variables holding full query results used only during page load, never referenced again after initial rendering. What is the correct fix?", options: [("A", "Increase the org's view state limit setting from 170 KB to a higher custom value in Setup."), ("B", "Convert the Visualforce page into a Lightning Web Component, since LWC has no equivalent capacity constraint of any kind."), ("C", "Mark those instance variables as transient, excluding them from the serialized view state sent between requests, since they are not needed for subsequent partial page refreshes."), ("D", "Remove the Standard Controller entirely, since only Custom Controllers are subject to view state limits.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Marking instance variables that are only needed during initial load (and never referenced in subsequent partial-page-refresh requests) as transient excludes them from the serialized view state, directly reducing its size and resolving the view state limit error. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The 170 KB view state limit is a fixed platform constraint; there is no Setup option to increase it to a custom, higher value.  \n* B: While LWC does not use the Visualforce view state mechanism, it has its own distinct performance and resource considerations; claiming \"no equivalent capacity constraint of any kind\" overstates this, and rewriting the page is a far larger change than the targeted, appropriate transient fix.  \n* D: View state limits apply to Visualforce pages regardless of whether they use a Standard Controller or Custom Controller; removing the Standard Controller does not exempt a page from this limit."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "Choose 2. A developer is deciding how to expose an LWC component so it appears as an option in Lightning App Builder for both Record Pages and App Pages, and separately wants a component usable inside a Flow screen. Which two target configuration facts are accurate?", options: [("A", "The .js-meta.xml file's <targets> section must declare lightning__RecordPage and lightning__AppPage for the component to appear as a placement option for those respective page types in Lightning App Builder."), ("B", "Declaring lightning__FlowScreen as a target makes the component usable as a screen component inside a Flow's Screen element."), ("C", "Components with no declared targets at all are automatically available in every possible placement context by default."), ("D", "lightning__RecordPage automatically grants the component the current record's Id without any additional @api property declaration."), ("E", "A single component's .js-meta.xml file can declare only one target at a time; declaring multiple targets requires separate component bundles."), ("F", "targetConfigs are mandatory for every target and cannot be omitted under any circumstances.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Declaring lightning__RecordPage and lightning__AppPage in the targets section makes the component available for those specific page-builder placement contexts, and declaring lightning__FlowScreen specifically enables the component to be used inside a Flow's Screen element — both are accurate, standard target configuration facts. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: Components with no declared targets are NOT automatically available everywhere; without an explicit target declaration, a component will not appear as a placement option in App Builder or Flow at all.  \n* D: While lightning__RecordPage does provide record context, the component must still explicitly declare an @api recordId property to receive and use that Id — it is not automatically granted with zero property declaration.  \n* E: A single .js-meta.xml file can declare multiple targets simultaneously (e.g., both lightning__RecordPage and lightning__AppPage together) within one component bundle; separate bundles are not required for multiple targets."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "A developer needs an LWC child component to expose a public property that a parent component sets declaratively via an HTML attribute, and this property must trigger reactive re-rendering in the child whenever the parent updates it. What decorator and pattern is correct?", options: [("A", "@api on the child's property (e.g., @api title;), set from the parent's template as an HTML attribute (<c-child title={parentTitle}>), which is reactive — when parentTitle changes in the parent, the child's title updates and re-renders automatically."), ("B", "@wire on the child's property, since @wire is required for any property set by a parent component via HTML attributes."), ("C", "@track on the child's property is mandatory in all modern LWC (API 45+) for any property to be settable from a parent, since @api alone is insufficient."), ("D", "The property must be set using a custom event dispatched from the parent to the child, since HTML attributes cannot pass data from parent to child in LWC.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: @api is specifically the decorator that exposes a component property as public-facing, settable via an HTML attribute from a parent template, and @api properties are reactive by default — exactly matching the described requirement with no additional decorators needed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: @wire connects a property or function to a data source (like an Apex method or a UI API wire adapter); it has no role in exposing a property for parent-to-child attribute binding — that is @api's specific purpose.  \n* C: @track is largely redundant in modern LWC since all properties are reactive by default for primitive reassignment; it is not a mandatory requirement layered on top of @api for parent-to-child data flow.  \n* D: HTML attributes are precisely how parent-to-child data flow works in LWC via @api properties; custom events are the mechanism for the opposite direction (child-to-parent communication), not parent-to-child."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "Choose 2. A developer is deciding between calling an Apex method imperatively versus using @wire in an LWC component that needs to: (1) load a list of records automatically when the component first renders, and (2) save a user's edited record only when they click a Save button. Which two tool choices correctly match each need?", options: [("A", "Use @wire for need (1), since @wire is designed for data that should load automatically and reactively refresh when its reactive parameters change."), ("B", "Use an imperative Apex call for need (2), since the save action is user-triggered (on a button click) and involves DML, which is incompatible with a cacheable=true wired method."), ("C", "Use @wire for need (2) as well, since @wire methods can perform DML as long as the button click passes the correct parameters."), ("D", "Use an imperative Apex call for need (1), since imperative calls are always required for any data that loads when a component renders."), ("E", "Both needs should use the exact same @wire property, since a single wire adapter can serve both automatic loading and user-triggered saving.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: @wire is the correct fit for automatically loading data on render (reactive, declarative), while an imperative call is required for the user-triggered save action since DML cannot be performed inside a cacheable=true wired method — correctly matching each need to the appropriate mechanism. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: @wire methods used with cacheable=true (required for @wire) are strictly read-only and cannot perform DML regardless of what parameters are passed; this option describes an impossible configuration.  \n* D: Imperative calls are not \"always required\" for data loaded on render — @wire is specifically designed for and commonly used for exactly this automatic, on-render loading scenario.  \n* E: A single @wire property cannot serve both an automatic reactive read and a user-triggered DML write; these are fundamentally different operations requiring separate methods (a cacheable wired method and a separate imperative method)."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "A developer's LWC component needs to notify its parent that a specific item was selected by the user, passing the selected item's Id as data, without the parent needing to poll or manually check the child's state. What is the correct implementation pattern?", options: [("A", "In the child, dispatch a custom event: this.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('itemselected', { detail: { itemId: this.selectedId } })); in the parent's template, listen with onitemselected={handleItemSelected}, and read event.detail.itemId in the handler."), ("B", "The parent should directly call a public method on the child via this.template.querySelector() every render cycle to poll for the current selection."), ("C", "The child should mutate an @api property on the parent directly, since @api properties are writable in both directions by any connected component."), ("D", "Use document.dispatchEvent() instead of this.dispatchEvent(), since document-level events are required for any parent-child LWC communication.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Dispatching a custom event from the child with a descriptive lowercase name and a detail payload, then listening for it in the parent's template via the on-prefixed attribute, is the standard, documented child-to-parent communication pattern in LWC — delivering the selected Id without any polling. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Polling via querySelector() on every render cycle is inefficient, fragile, and not the idiomatic LWC communication pattern; event dispatching provides immediate, push-based notification instead.  \n* C: @api properties are designed for one-way parent-to-child data flow; children should never directly mutate a parent's properties, and @api does not support that reverse-direction mutation pattern.  \n* D: this.dispatchEvent() dispatched from the component itself (not document) is the correct, standard mechanism; using document.dispatchEvent() would bypass the component's own event system and violate the intended encapsulated communication model."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs two LWC components that are siblings (not directly nested) on the same Lightning Record Page to communicate: selecting a filter in Component A should update the displayed data in Component B, and neither component has a shared parent that both are children of within the same custom component tree. Which two approaches correctly solve this?", options: [("A", "Use Lightning Message Service (LMS) with a defined MessageChannel: Component A publishes a message via publish(), and Component B subscribes via subscribe(), enabling communication across the page regardless of DOM hierarchy."), ("B", "If both components happen to share a common ancestor container that manages state, that ancestor can catch an event from Component A and update an @api property passed down to Component"), ("D", "Custom events dispatched from Component A will automatically bubble across the entire Lightning page to reach any sibling component with zero configuration."), ("E", "Use document.querySelector() to locate Component B's DOM node from Component A and call an internal method directly.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Lightning Message Service is specifically designed for exactly this scenario — decoupled communication across any DOM hierarchy on the page — and if a genuine shared ancestor/container exists that can catch an event and pass data down via @api, that pattern also correctly solves the sibling communication problem. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: this.template.querySelector() can only reach within a component's OWN shadow DOM; it cannot reach into a sibling component's internal shadow DOM to set properties, due to Shadow DOM/Locker Service isolation.  \n* D: Custom events do not automatically bubble across the entire page to arbitrary siblings by default; achieving broader propagation requires explicit bubbles/composed configuration and even then depends on actual DOM ancestry, not just page presence.  \n* E: document.querySelector() violates Locker Service's shadow DOM isolation model and is explicitly the kind of dangerous, boundary-crossing access the sandbox is designed to prevent; it is not a valid or supported communication pattern."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "A developer's LWC component uses @wire(getRecord, {recordId: '$recordId', fields: [NAME_FIELD]}) but the wire never re-fires when recordId changes after the initial render. What is the likely cause?", options: [("A", "The $ prefix is missing from the actual property reference in code (it should reference the reactive property, e.g., '$recordId', exactly as shown) — without the $ prefix, Salesforce treats the value as a static literal rather than a reactive reference, so changes to recordId never trigger a re-fetch."), ("B", "@wire adapters can never be reactive under any circumstances; a full manual re-render trigger is always required."), ("C", "getRecord specifically does not support reactive parameters, unlike all other wire adapters."), ("D", "The fields array must also use the $ prefix on every field reference for reactivity to work.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: The $ prefix is what marks a property reference as reactive in a @wire configuration; if recordId is referenced without the $ prefix (as a static value instead), the wire will never detect changes to it and will not automatically re-fetch data when it changes. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: @wire adapters absolutely can be reactive — this is one of their core, defining features when properties are correctly referenced with the $ prefix; claiming no reactivity is possible is factually incorrect.  \n* C: getRecord fully supports reactive parameters like recordId via the $ prefix; this is a standard, common, documented usage pattern, not an exception.  \n* D: The $ prefix is applied to reactive property references used as parameter values (like recordId); field API name imports (like NAME_FIELD) in the fields array are static schema references and do not use or need the $ prefix."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "Choose 2. A developer building an LWC component needs to navigate programmatically to a specific Account record's detail page after a successful save action. Which two facts about NavigationMixin are accurate?", options: [("A", "The component class must extend NavigationMixin(LightningElement) after importing NavigationMixin from 'lightning/navigation'."), ("B", "Navigation is triggered by calling thisNavigationMixin.Navigate."), ("C", "NavigationMixin can only navigate to standard object pages; it has no support for navigating to a specific record's detail page."), ("D", "Hardcoding the record's URL as a string (e.g., '/lightning/r/Account/' + accountId + '/view') is the Salesforce-recommended best practice over using NavigationMixin."), ("E", "NavigationMixin.Navigate is a standalone function that can be called directly without extending the component class with NavigationMixin.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: NavigationMixin must be imported and used to extend the component class, and navigation is triggered by calling thisNavigationMixin.Navigate with a page reference object specifying the type (standard__recordPage) and attributes (recordId, actionName) — both are accurate, standard implementation facts. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: NavigationMixin fully supports navigating to a specific record's detail page via the standard__recordPage page type with a recordId attribute; this is one of its most common use cases, not an unsupported one.  \n* D: Salesforce explicitly recommends using NavigationMixin over hardcoded URL strings, since hardcoded URLs are fragile, environment-dependent, and bypass the platform's navigation service.  \n* E: thisNavigationMixin.Navigate must be called on a component instance that has extended NavigationMixin(LightningElement); it is not a standalone function usable without that extension."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "A developer needs to read a URL query parameter (e.g., a campaign source code) when an LWC component loads on a standalone App Page, reactively responding if the parameter changes without a full page reload. What is the correct approach?", options: [("A", "Use @wire(CurrentPageReference) from 'lightning/navigation', then extract the value via a getter reading this.pageRef?.state?.myParam, since CurrentPageReference's state property holds URL query parameters and the wire is reactive to URL changes."), ("B", "Use window.location.search directly in connectedCallback(), since this is the Salesforce-recommended, Locker Service-compliant approach for reading URL parameters in LWC."), ("C", "URL query parameters cannot be read in LWC under any circumstances; this requires a Visualforce page instead."), ("D", "Use @api urlParams; and Salesforce will automatically populate it with all URL query parameters with no additional configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: @wire(CurrentPageReference) is the correct, reactive, Locker Service-compliant mechanism for reading page reference data including URL state (query parameters) in LWC, and since it's a wire adapter, it reactively updates the component if the underlying page reference changes. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Directly accessing window.location is discouraged and can conflict with Locker Service's sandboxing model; CurrentPageReference is the Salesforce-recommended, supported approach specifically for this purpose.  \n* C: LWC fully supports reading URL parameters via the CurrentPageReference wire adapter; claiming it's impossible and requires Visualforce is factually incorrect.  \n* D: There is no @api urlParams magic property that Salesforce automatically populates; developers must explicitly wire CurrentPageReference and extract values from its state property themselves."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "Choose 2. A developer is deciding between lightning-record-form, lightning-record-edit-form, and lightning-record-view-form for a new feature requiring highly customized field layout with conditional field visibility logic driven by component JavaScript. Which two facts correctly inform this decision?", options: [("A", "lightning-record-form is the simplest option, auto-generating a view or edit form based on page layout or a field list, but offers the least layout customization control."), ("B", "lightning-record-edit-form paired with lightning-input-field components gives the developer full control over field layout and submission logic, making it suitable for conditional field visibility driven by custom JavaScript."), ("C", "lightning-record-view-form is the only option that supports any form of field-level customization; both other form types render every field identically with zero flexibility."), ("D", "lightning-record-form always provides more granular layout control than lightning-record-edit-form, making it the better choice for this specific requirement."), ("E", "All three form components require an explicit Apex controller method to load record data; none of them use the UI API directly.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: lightning-record-form is indeed the simplest but least customizable option, while lightning-record-edit-form with lightning-input-field components provides the fine-grained layout control and custom submission logic needed for conditional field visibility — correctly informing the choice toward the edit-form approach for this requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* C: lightning-record-edit-form (using lightning-input-field) also supports meaningful field-level customization and conditional rendering; view-form is not uniquely capable of this among the three options.  \n* D: This reverses the actual relationship — lightning-record-form offers LESS granular control than lightning-record-edit-form, not more, which is why the edit-form approach is better suited to this highly customized requirement.  \n* E: All three of these Lightning Data Service-based components use the UI API directly under the hood; none of them require an explicit custom Apex controller method to load record data."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "A developer needs to update a Contact record's fields directly from LWC JavaScript logic after some client-side validation, without writing a custom Apex method, while respecting the running user's FLS and sharing rules automatically. What is the correct approach?", options: [("A", "Import updateRecord from 'lightning/uiRecordApi' and call updateRecord({ fields: { Id: this.recordId, LastName: 'Jones' } }); this method uses the UI API, which respects FLS and sharing, and automatically refreshes any wired data affected by the change."), ("B", "Write a custom @AuraEnabled(cacheable=true) Apex method that performs the update, since cacheable=true methods are specifically designed for DML operations initiated from LWC."), ("C", "Directly manipulate the this.recordId property's underlying database row from JavaScript, since LWC has direct database access without any API layer."), ("D", "Use @wire(getRecord) to both read and write the record, since getRecord supports a two-way binding mode for edits.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: updateRecord from lightning/uiRecordApi is specifically designed for exactly this scenario — performing simple record updates directly from LWC JavaScript without custom Apex, while using the UI API to respect FLS and sharing rules and automatically refreshing related wired data. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: cacheable=true methods are strictly read-only and explicitly cannot perform DML; this is the opposite of what's needed and would throw a runtime exception if DML were attempted.  \n* C: LWC has no direct database access whatsoever; all data operations go through defined APIs (UI API, Apex) that enforce security and business logic — there is no bypass mechanism.  \n* D: getRecord is a read-only wire adapter for retrieving record data reactively; it has no \"two-way binding\" write capability, and updates must go through updateRecord or Apex DML instead."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "A developer needs an @AuraEnabled Apex method that, when called from LWC, throws a specific, user-friendly error message (not a generic \"internal server error\") when a business validation fails. What is the correct pattern?", options: [("A", "Simply return null from the method when validation fails; LWC will automatically interpret a null return as a specific validation error message."), ("B", "throw new DmlException('message'); since DmlException always surfaces its exact message text to LWC regardless of context."), ("C", "throw new AuraHandledException('Specific business validation failed: discount exceeds policy limit.'); since AuraHandledException is specifically designed to surface a readable message to LWC/Aura via error.body.message in the catch block, unlike a generic Apex exception which returns an opaque message."), ("D", "System.debug() the error message instead of throwing anything, since LWC automatically displays all debug log output to the end user.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: AuraHandledException is purpose-built for this exact scenario — when thrown from an @AuraEnabled method, its message is surfaced to LWC/Aura via error.body.message in the catch block, giving the user a specific, readable message instead of a generic \"internal server error.\" Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Returning null provides no information about what went wrong and gives LWC nothing to distinguish a validation failure from any other null-result scenario; there's no automatic interpretation of null as a specific error message.  \n* B: Throwing a standard exception type like DmlException from an @AuraEnabled method still results in LWC receiving a generic, non-specific error message unless it's specifically wrapped as or replaced by an AuraHandledException.  \n* D: System.debug() writes to the server-side debug log only; it has no mechanism to surface any message to the end user in the browser, and LWC has no automatic access to debug logs."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "Choose 2. A developer is deciding whether a new UI requirement — a fully custom, real-time collaborative drawing canvas embedded in a record page — can be built declaratively or requires LWC. Which two facts correctly justify the need for programmatic (LWC) development?", options: [("A", "Since the requirement touches a record page, it must be built using only declarative Lightning App Builder configuration by definition, regardless of the complexity of the requested UI behavior."), ("B", "Any UI requirement involving JavaScript automatically disqualifies it from LWC and instead requires a Visualforce page."), ("C", "A highly custom, non-standard interactive visualization or widget that no standard or Flow-based component can render is a genuine scenario requiring custom LWC development."), ("D", "Standard Lightning components and Flow screens do not offer real-time collaborative drawing canvas functionality; this level of fully custom, interactive, non-standard UI exceeds their built-in capabilities."), ("E", "A Screen Flow's built-in Display Text component can be configured to support real-time collaborative drawing with a single configuration checkbox.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Standard components and Flow screens have no built-in support for a real-time collaborative drawing canvas, and this is exactly the kind of highly custom, non-standard interactive visualization that genuinely requires custom LWC development rather than declarative configuration. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Being placed on a record page does not restrict a requirement to purely declarative configuration; record pages fully support custom LWC components for exactly these kinds of advanced requirements.  \n* B: LWC is a JavaScript-based framework by definition; requiring JavaScript is not a disqualifying factor for LWC — quite the opposite, it is LWC's core technology.  \n* E: There is no such built-in Display Text configuration option for real-time collaborative drawing in Screen Flow; this describes a nonexistent capability."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "A developer's LWC component needs to conditionally render one of three different sections based on a status property's value (\"Draft\", \"Submitted\", \"Approved\"), using the modern LWC templating approach available in current API versions. What is the correct syntax pattern?", options: [("A", "Use three separate, entirely independent LWC components with no shared template logic, since LWC cannot conditionally render sections within a single template."), ("B", "Conditional rendering is not supported in LWC templates under any circumstances; all three sections must always render simultaneously."), ("C", "<template if:true={status}> for each of the three sections, relying on status being truthy in all three cases simultaneously."), ("D", "<template lwc:if={isDraft}><!-- Draft UI --></template><template lwc:elseif={isSubmitted}><!-- Submitted UI --></template><template lwc:else><!-- Approved UI --></template>, using getters (isDraft, isSubmitted) that derive Boolean values from the status property.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: The lwc:if / lwc:elseif / lwc:else directive set (the current, modern conditional rendering syntax) correctly handles mutually exclusive branching across three possible states, using getters that derive Boolean conditions from the status property — exactly matching this three-way conditional requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: LWC fully supports conditional rendering of multiple sections within a single component's template via the lwc:if/elseif/else (or legacy if:true/if:false) directives; splitting into three separate components is unnecessary and not a required workaround.  \n* B: Conditional rendering is a core, well-supported LWC templating feature; claiming it's unsupported is factually incorrect.  \n* C: if:true={status} would only correctly branch on a single Boolean-like truthy check of the raw status value; it cannot cleanly express three distinct, mutually exclusive string-based conditions the way lwc:if/elseif/else can with dedicated getters."),
        Question(id: "74", question: "Choose 2. A developer is building an LWC component that must iterate over a list of Opportunity records and render one row per record, and needs to understand the required for:each syntax and its key requirement. Which two facts are accurate?", options: [("A", "Using a record's unique Id field (e.g., opp.Id) as the key value is a standard, recommended practice, since keys must be unique and stable across re-renders."), ("B", "for:each can only iterate over arrays of primitive values (like strings or numbers), never arrays of objects like Opportunity records."), ("C", "The for:each directive requires a for:item attribute naming the iteration variable, and the immediate child element inside the loop must have a unique key attribute (e.g., key={opp.Id}) for efficient DOM diffing."), ("D", "The for:index attribute is mandatory and must always be used alongside for:item in every for:each loop."), ("E", "The key attribute is optional and can be omitted safely with zero impact on rendering correctness or performance.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: for:each requires for:item to name the loop variable and a unique key attribute on the immediate child for efficient DOM diffing, and using a record's stable, unique Id field as that key is exactly the standard, recommended practice for this purpose. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: for:each fully supports iterating over arrays of objects (like a list of Opportunity records), which is in fact its most common use case; it is not restricted to primitive value arrays.  \n* D: for:index is optional and only needed when the index value itself is required in the template logic; it is not mandatory alongside for:item in every loop.  \n* E: Omitting the key attribute is not safe — Salesforce requires and validates for a key on the immediate child of a for:each loop; missing it causes a template compilation error or degraded rendering behavior."),
        Question(id: "75", question: "A developer needs an LWC component's lifecycle hook to run cleanup logic (unsubscribing from a Lightning Message Service channel) exactly once, when the component is removed from the page. Which lifecycle hook is correct, and why not renderedCallback()?", options: [("A", "disconnectedCallback(), since it fires specifically when the component is removed from the DOM, making it the correct place for cleanup like unsubscribing from a message channel; renderedCallback() fires after every render cycle and is not tied to component removal."), ("B", "renderedCallback(), since it is the only hook guaranteed to fire exactly once during a component's entire lifecycle."), ("C", "errorCallback(), since all cleanup logic must be placed inside error handling hooks regardless of whether an error actually occurred."), ("D", "connectedCallback(), since cleanup logic should always run when a component is first inserted into the DOM, not when it's removed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: disconnectedCallback() is specifically designed to fire when a component is removed from the DOM, making it the correct, purpose-built location for cleanup logic like unsubscribing from a Lightning Message Service channel; renderedCallback() fires after every render (potentially many times) and has no connection to component removal. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: renderedCallback() is not guaranteed to fire only once — it fires after every render cycle, potentially many times throughout a component's life, making it unsuitable and risky for one-time cleanup logic.  \n* C: errorCallback() fires only when a child component throws an error; it is not a general-purpose cleanup hook and has no relationship to normal component removal or subscription cleanup.  \n* D: connectedCallback() fires on insertion into the DOM (component startup), which is the opposite lifecycle point from removal; cleanup logic tied to removal belongs in disconnectedCallback(), not connectedCallback()."),
        Question(id: "76", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to decide between lightning-record-form and a fully custom LWC using getRecord/updateRecord for displaying and editing an Opportunity record, given a requirement for a highly branded, non-standard visual layout with custom validation messaging beyond what standard components provide. Which two considerations correctly favor the fully custom approach?", options: [("A", "lightning-record-form is legally required for any Opportunity-related UI due to platform restrictions unique to that object."), ("B", "Custom validation messaging beyond standard field-level error display requires custom JavaScript logic and markup, which a fully custom LWC component can implement, unlike the more constrained standard form components."), ("C", "Standard form components and fully custom LWC implementations are functionally and visually identical, so this decision has no real impact."), ("D", "A fully custom LWC using getRecord/updateRecord cannot access or display any Opportunity field data under any circumstances."), ("E", "lightning-record-form auto-generates its layout based on page layout or a field list, offering limited control over highly custom branding and visual structure compared to hand-built markup using getRecord/getFieldValue and custom CSS.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Why correct: lightning-record-form's auto-generated layout offers less visual/branding control than hand-built markup, and implementing custom validation messaging beyond standard field-level errors requires custom JavaScript logic that a fully custom component can provide — both genuinely justify the more custom approach for this requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no such platform restriction requiring lightning-record-form specifically for Opportunity; any standard object can use either approach.  \n* C: These approaches differ significantly in flexibility, control, and visual outcome; claiming no real impact ignores the very real trade-offs between simplicity (record-form) and customization (fully custom LWC).  \n* D: A fully custom LWC using getRecord and getFieldValue can absolutely access and display any Opportunity field data (subject to FLS); this claim of zero access is factually incorrect."),
        Question(id: "77", question: "A developer needs to expose a Visualforce page as an override for the standard \"New\" action on the Case object, while still leveraging the Standard Controller's automatic record loading and save behavior. What configuration is correct?", options: [("A", "Build the Visualforce page with standardController='Case', configure it in Setup as an override for the New button/action on Case, and rely on the Standard Controller's built-in save/cancel actions bound via action='{!save}' and action='{!cancel}'."), ("B", "Overriding the New action requires disabling the Case object's standard page layouts entirely."), ("C", "Build the page with a fully custom controller (controller='MyCustomController') with no standardController attribute, since standard action overrides are incompatible with Standard Controllers."), ("D", "Standard action overrides can only be configured for Lightning Web Components, never for Visualforce pages.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Using standardController='Case' provides automatic record context and built-in save/cancel actions, and Visualforce pages using a Standard Controller are a fully supported, standard mechanism for overriding actions like New in Setup — exactly matching this requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Overriding a standard action like New has no requirement to disable page layouts; page layouts and action overrides are independent, complementary configuration areas.  \n* C: Standard action overrides work perfectly well with Standard Controllers (and Controller Extensions); a fully custom controller with no standardController attribute is not a requirement and would actually lose the automatic record-loading and save/cancel behavior the scenario wants to leverage.  \n* D: Visualforce pages have long been a supported mechanism for overriding standard actions (New, Edit, View, Tab); this capability is not restricted to Lightning Web Components."),
        Question(id: "78", question: "Choose 2. A developer is building a Visualforce page using apex:pageBlockTable bound to a Standard List Controller for Accounts, and needs pagination controls. Which two facts about the Standard List Controller's pagination capabilities are accurate?", options: [("A", "The default page size for a Standard List Controller is 2,000 records and cannot be changed."), ("B", "The action attributes {!previous}, {!next}, {!first}, and {!last} are available on the Standard List Controller and can be bound to command buttons to page through the record set."), ("C", "Standard List Controllers do not support any built-in pagination; all pagination logic must be manually implemented in a custom controller."), ("D", "recordSetVar is unnecessary and has no effect on how the Standard List Controller behaves."), ("E", "hasNext and hasPrevious are Boolean properties on the Standard List Controller useful for conditionally rendering or disabling pagination buttons.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Why correct: The Standard List Controller natively provides the previous/next/first/last navigation actions bindable to buttons, and hasNext/hasPrevious are built-in Boolean properties specifically useful for conditionally showing or disabling those pagination controls — both are accurate, native capabilities. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The default page size is actually 20 records, not 2,000, and it IS configurable via the pageSize attribute on the Standard List Controller configuration.  \n* C: Standard List Controllers do provide built-in pagination support (via recordSetVar and the described action attributes); a fully custom controller with manually implemented pagination is not required to achieve this.  \n* D: recordSetVar is specifically what activates the Standard List Controller behavior (as opposed to a single-record Standard Controller); it has a significant, defining effect on the controller's behavior, not none."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "A developer needs a Visualforce page component that calls a controller method automatically every 30 seconds to refresh a dashboard-style display, without requiring the user to click any button. What is the correct component, and what is a key consideration?", options: [("A", "<apex:actionPoller action=\"{!refreshData}\" interval=\"30\" reRender=\"dataPanel\"/>, which calls refreshData() every 30 seconds and re-renders dataPanel; a key consideration is that the poller continues firing even when the user is idle, generating ongoing server load, so a way to stop it should be considered."), ("B", "The minimum allowed polling interval is 1 second, so setting interval=\"30\" is unnecessarily conservative and should be reduced."), ("C", "<apex:actionSupport event=\"onchange\" action=\"{!refreshData}\"/>, since actionSupport is specifically designed for time-based, interval-driven polling."), ("D", "<apex:actionFunction name=\"refreshData\"/> alone, since actionFunction automatically polls on a fixed interval with no additional configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: apex:actionPoller is specifically designed for time-interval-based automatic controller method calls, and a genuine, well-known consideration is that it keeps firing regardless of user activity, generating continuous server load unless intentionally stopped. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: The documented minimum interval for apex:actionPoller is 5 seconds, not 1 second; setting interval=\"30\" is a reasonable, valid configuration well above that minimum, not unnecessarily conservative relative to an incorrect 1-second floor.  \n* C: apex:actionSupport fires a controller action in response to a DOM event (like onchange or onclick), not on a time interval; it has no built-in polling/interval capability.  \n* D: apex:actionFunction generates a callable JavaScript function invoked explicitly from other JavaScript code; it has no automatic, built-in polling behavior on its own."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to embed a Flow inside a Visualforce page, passing an input variable from an Apex-bound property, and redirecting the user to a different page once the flow finishes. Which two facts about the component are accurate?", options: [("A", "Input variables passed via are matched to Flow variables by position (order) rather than by name."), ("B", "The finishLocation attribute specifies where to redirect the user once the embedded flow interview completes."), ("C", "The name attribute of references the Flow's API name using underscores (not spaces), and nested elements pass input variables whose name attribute must exactly match the Flow's input variable name."), ("D", "The Flow referenced by always runs under the page's Standard Controller's user context rather than the actual logged-in user's context."), ("E", "can only embed Screen Flows that have zero input or output variables defined.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "Why correct: The name attribute correctly references the Flow's API name (with underscores), input variables are passed via matched by exact name to the Flow's input variables, and finishLocation correctly specifies the post-completion redirect target — both statements accurately describe configuration. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Input variables passed via are matched by name (matching the param's name attribute to the Flow's input variable name), not by positional order.  \n* D: The Flow runs in the actual logged-in user's context (respecting their permissions), not a separate \"Standard Controller's user context\" concept, which isn't how Flow execution context works.  \n* E: can embed Screen Flows that do have input and output variables defined; passing input variables via apex:param and using finishLocation for redirection are exactly the mechanisms used when a flow does have variables.\n\n# Testing, Debugging, and Deployment"),
        Question(id: "81", question: "A developer's new trigger has zero test coverage and the team is preparing to deploy to production. What minimum code coverage requirement must be met, and at what granularity is it measured?", options: [("A", "Each individual class and trigger must independently reach at least 75% coverage; the org-wide aggregate percentage is irrelevant."), ("B", "At least 75% code coverage is required across all Apex classes and triggers in the org in aggregate (not per individual class), with the additional requirement that each individual trigger have at least 1% coverage."), ("C", "There is no minimum coverage requirement for deploying to production; coverage is purely a best-practice recommendation with no enforcement."), ("D", "100% coverage is mandatory for every class and trigger with zero exceptions before any production deployment is allowed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce requires at least 75% code coverage measured in aggregate across all Apex classes and triggers in the org (not per individual class), with the additional specific requirement that each individual trigger have at least 1% coverage — this is the actual, documented enforcement rule for production deployments. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The 75% requirement is calculated in aggregate across the org, not as an independent per-class/per-trigger minimum; individual classes can be below 75% as long as the overall aggregate meets the threshold (though best practice still aims higher per class).  \n* C: This is factually incorrect — Salesforce actively enforces the 75% aggregate coverage requirement, and a deployment to production will fail if this threshold isn't met.  \n* D: 100% coverage is not a platform-enforced requirement; 75% aggregate is the enforced minimum, though many teams aim higher as a best practice."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "Choose 2. A developer's test method asserts System.assertEquals(expectedTotal, actualTotal); but includes no message parameter, making failures hard to diagnose. Which two improvements reflect proper assertion best practices?", options: [("A", "Ensure tests contain meaningful assertions on actual outcomes, not just verification that no exception was thrown, since code coverage without real assertions provides false confidence in correctness."), ("B", "Always use System.assert(true) at the end of every test method as a substitute for specific value-based assertions."), ("C", "Remove all assertions from test methods entirely once code coverage exceeds 75%, since coverage percentage is the only metric that matters for test quality."), ("D", "Add an optional message parameter: System.assertEquals(expectedTotal, actualTotal, 'Total should equal sum of line item amounts'); so a failure clearly states what was expected, saving debugging time."), ("E", "Replace all assertions with System.debug() statements instead, since debug logs are a superior mechanism for verifying expected test outcomes.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Adding a descriptive message parameter to assertions makes failures immediately diagnosable, and ensuring tests actually assert meaningful outcomes (not just that code executed without throwing) is essential test quality practice — coverage percentage alone says nothing about whether the code's behavior was actually verified. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: System.assert(true) is a no-op assertion that will never fail and verifies nothing meaningful about the actual code behavior; it is the opposite of a genuine, value-based correctness check.  \n* C: Removing assertions once coverage is met defeats the entire purpose of testing — coverage measures which lines executed, not whether the code behaved correctly; quality tests need meaningful assertions regardless of coverage percentage.  \n* E: System.debug() statements only write to a log for manual inspection; they do not cause a test to fail when an unexpected value occurs, unlike a proper assertion, making them a poor substitute for verifying correctness."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "A test class needs shared test data (an Account and related Contacts) available to five different test methods, without duplicating the record-creation code in each method and without those methods interfering with each other's data state. What is the correct approach?", options: [("A", "Store the shared data in static class-level variables initialized once when the class is first loaded, shared and mutated freely across all five test methods without any rollback between them."), ("B", "Use @isTest(SeeAllData=true) to pull in existing production Account and Contact records instead of creating new test-specific data."), ("C", "Use @TestSetup on a static void method that creates the shared Account and Contacts once; this data is automatically rolled back after each test method and restored to its original state before the next test method runs, ensuring isolation."), ("D", "Create the shared data inside a regular instance method called manually at the start of each of the five test methods, duplicating the setup code five times for reliability.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: @TestSetup is specifically designed for exactly this scenario — it creates shared data once, and Salesforce automatically rolls back and restores that data to its original state before each individual test method runs, giving all five methods the same starting data without duplication or cross-method interference. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Static variables shared across test methods with no rollback mechanism between them risk one test's mutations corrupting the data seen by a later test, directly undermining test isolation.  \n* B: SeeAllData=true introduces dependency on real, unpredictable production data, which is exactly what should be avoided for deterministic, isolated tests; it does not address the shared-setup-without-duplication requirement at all.  \n* D: Duplicating setup code in an instance method called five times directly contradicts the goal of avoiding duplication, and provides no protection against one test's mutations affecting a later test within the same method (whereas @TestSetup resets between methods)."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "Choose 2. A developer is testing a method that enqueues a Queueable job and separately schedules a nightly Batch Apex job, and needs both to actually execute synchronously within the test so assertions can run against their results. Which two facts about Test.startTest()/Test.stopTest() are accurate?", options: [("A", "Async jobs enqueued between startTest and stopTest continue running asynchronously in the background even after the test method itself completes and returns."), ("B", "Test.startTest() resets the governor limit counters for the code that follows, giving the code under test a clean set of limits separate from any setup code that ran before it."), ("C", "Test.stopTest() must be called before Test.startTest() in the code, since the platform executes them in reverse declaration order."), ("D", "Any asynchronous operations (Queueable jobs, Batch Apex, @future calls) enqueued between Test.startTest() and Test.stopTest() are forced to execute synchronously at the moment Test.stopTest() is called."), ("E", "Governor limits are only relevant inside Test.startTest()/Test.stopTest(); code outside that block is entirely exempt from all governor limits during a test run.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Test.stopTest() forces any async operations enqueued since startTest() to run synchronously at that point (allowing immediate assertions on their results), and Test.startTest() resets governor limit counters, giving the code under test a clean slate separate from setup code — both are accurate, foundational facts about this testing pattern. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Async jobs forced to run at Test.stopTest() complete synchronously at that point within the test itself; they do not continue running in the background after the test method returns.  \n* C: Test.startTest() must be called before Test.stopTest() in the code, in that literal order; there is no \"reverse declaration order\" execution behavior.  \n* E: Governor limits still apply to code outside the startTest/stopTest block (such as setup code); the block specifically provides a fresh reset of limit counters for the code under test, not a blanket exemption from all limits everywhere in the test."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "A developer's test needs to verify that a with sharing class correctly restricts a user without appropriate sharing access from seeing records owned by another user. What is the correct testing pattern?", options: [("A", "Create a test user with an appropriately restrictive profile, then wrap the relevant test logic in System.runAs(testUser) { /* logic and assertions here */ }, simulating that user's session to verify the with sharing class properly enforces restricted visibility."), ("B", "Use @isTest(SeeAllData=true) to borrow an existing real user's session context instead of creating a dedicated test user."), ("C", "System.runAs() automatically bypasses all sharing rules regardless of the wrapped user's actual profile, making it unsuitable for this kind of test."), ("D", "Testing sharing rules and profile-based restrictions is impossible in Apex tests; this must always be verified through manual UI testing only.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: System.runAs(testUser) is specifically designed to simulate a given user's session within a test, and wrapping the relevant logic and assertions inside it correctly verifies that a with sharing class enforces the expected restricted visibility for a user with a particular profile/sharing context. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: SeeAllData=true relates to accessing real org data, not to simulating a specific user's session context; it is unrelated to and doesn't replace the need for System.runAs() when testing user-specific sharing behavior.  \n* C: System.runAs() does not bypass sharing rules for the wrapped user's own profile-based restrictions; it specifically simulates that user's actual context, which is exactly why it's the correct tool for verifying with sharing enforcement.  \n* D: Apex tests fully support verifying sharing and profile-based restrictions programmatically via System.runAs(); manual UI testing is not the only, or even the primary, way to validate this behavior."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "Choose 2. A developer's Apex class makes an HTTP callout to an external shipping API, and the corresponding test method needs to run without making a real network call, since real callouts are blocked in test context. Which two facts about mocking callouts are accurate?", options: [("A", "Apex tests automatically simulate a successful HTTP 200 response for any callout with zero mock configuration required."), ("B", "Test.setMock() must be called after the callout has already executed, since it retroactively substitutes the response that was already received."), ("C", "StaticResourceCalloutMock is a built-in alternative to a custom HttpCalloutMock implementation, using a static resource file's content as the mocked response body."), ("D", "Real HTTP callouts are permitted during Apex test execution as long as Test.startTest() has been called first."), ("E", "Implementing the HttpCalloutMock interface (with a respond(HTTPRequest req) method returning a constructed HTTPResponse) and registering it via Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, new MyMock()) is the standard pattern for mocking HTTP callouts in tests.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 4], explanation: "Why correct: Implementing HttpCalloutMock with Test.setMock() is the standard, documented pattern for mocking HTTP callouts, and StaticResourceCalloutMock is a genuine, built-in alternative that uses a static resource's content as the mock response — both accurately describe supported callout mocking mechanisms. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no automatic, zero-configuration simulated success response; without an explicitly registered mock, a callout attempted during a test throws an exception because real callouts aren't permitted.  \n* B: Test.setMock() must be called BEFORE the callout executes (typically before Test.startTest() or right after, but always before the actual callout code runs), since it registers the mock that will intercept the upcoming callout — it cannot retroactively apply after the fact.  \n* D: Real HTTP callouts are blocked in Apex test execution regardless of whether Test.startTest() was called; this is a fundamental platform restriction for test determinism, not something startTest() lifts."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "A Flow requires deployment coverage similar to Apex before it can be promoted to production. What constitutes coverage for a Flow, according to Salesforce's testing model?", options: [("A", "Flows have no concept of test coverage whatsoever; they are exempt from any coverage requirement for deployment."), ("B", "Flow coverage can only be achieved by writing a fully separate Apex test class with zero interaction with the Flow itself."), ("C", "Flow coverage is calculated identically to Apex line coverage, requiring a minimum of 75% of Flow elements to execute during any test run."), ("D", "Flow coverage is achieved by running flow interviews from Apex tests (e.g., triggering record DML that fires a record-triggered flow, or invoking an autolaunched flow via Flow.Interview) or by using Flow Test scenarios in Flow Builder that define input values and expected outcomes, run to verify at least one logical path.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Flow coverage is achieved either by running the flow interview from Apex tests (via record DML that triggers it, or Flow.Interview for autolaunched flows) or through Flow Builder's own Flow Test feature defining scenarios with inputs and expected outcomes — both are legitimate, documented mechanisms for establishing flow coverage. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Flows are not universally exempt from coverage requirements for deployment; depending on context and org settings, flow testing/coverage considerations do apply, particularly as Salesforce has increasingly emphasized flow test scenarios.  \n* B: A flow test does not require zero interaction with the flow itself — quite the opposite, the whole point is to actually exercise the flow's logic, either by triggering it via Apex or through Flow Builder's own test scenario runner.  \n* C: Flow coverage is not calculated identically to Apex's line-based 75% aggregate model; it is verified through actual interview execution and defined test scenarios rather than a percentage-of-elements-executed calculation."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to decide when Test.isRunningTest() is an appropriate tool to use inside production Apex code. Which two considerations correctly describe its appropriate and inappropriate use?", options: [("A", "Using Test.isRunningTest() to bypass a real callout instead of properly mocking it with Test.setMock() means that code path receives zero genuine test coverage and should generally be avoided in favor of proper mocking."), ("B", "Test.isRunningTest() is the Salesforce-recommended primary mechanism for handling all callouts in test contexts, replacing the need for Test.setMock() entirely."), ("C", "Test.isRunningTest() returns false inside any test method, since it only detects non-test execution contexts."), ("D", "Test.isRunningTest() returns true when executing code is running inside a test context, and it can be used sparingly to skip code paths that are genuinely impossible to test otherwise (like certain platform-specific behaviors)."), ("E", "Test.isRunningTest() can be used to change core business logic behavior between production and test execution as a normal, encouraged development pattern.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3], explanation: "Why correct: Test.isRunningTest() does return true in a test context and has a narrow, legitimate use for truly untestable edge cases, but relying on it to bypass real logic (like a callout) instead of proper mocking means that path is never genuinely exercised by tests — both facts correctly frame it as a sparingly-used tool, not a primary testing strategy. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Test.setMock() (with HttpCalloutMock) is the Salesforce-recommended, standard mechanism for handling callouts in tests; Test.isRunningTest() bypassing callout logic entirely is a discouraged shortcut, not a replacement for proper mocking.  \n* C: Test.isRunningTest() returns TRUE (not false) when code is executing inside a test context; this option describes the exact opposite of its actual behavior.  \n* E: Using Test.isRunningTest() to change core business logic behavior between environments is explicitly discouraged, since it means production code behaves differently than what is actually tested, undermining confidence that tests reflect real production behavior."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "A developer needs to write an Apex test that verifies a Visualforce controller extension correctly reads a URL parameter and populates a property. What setup is required in the test method?", options: [("A", "Use System.runAs() to simulate the URL parameter being present, since runAs() is designed for both user context and URL parameter simulation."), ("B", "URL parameters cannot be simulated in Apex tests under any circumstances; this scenario always requires a Selenium-based UI test instead."), ("C", "Simply instantiate the controller extension directly with no special setup, since URL parameters are automatically available to any Apex test with zero configuration."), ("D", "PageReference pageRef = Page.MyVFPage; Test.setCurrentPage(pageRef); pageRef.getParameters().put('id', testRecord.Id); then instantiate the Standard Controller and extension — Test.setCurrentPage() is required so the controller extension's code (using ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters()) can actually read the simulated URL parameter during the test.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Test.setCurrentPage() combined with setting parameters on the PageReference is specifically required so that any code in the controller extension reading ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters() during the test actually sees the simulated URL parameter value, correctly setting up this test scenario. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: System.runAs() is specifically for simulating a different user's session context (for sharing/permission testing); it has no relationship to or capability for simulating URL parameters.  \n* B: URL parameters absolutely can be simulated in a pure Apex unit test via Test.setCurrentPage() and PageReference parameters; a full UI-based Selenium test is not required for this kind of controller logic verification.  \n* C: Without Test.setCurrentPage() and explicitly setting the parameter, ApexPages.currentPage().getParameters() would not contain the expected value during the test; there is no automatic, zero-configuration availability of URL parameters in a test."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "Choose 2. A developer is building a test data factory pattern to reduce duplication across multiple test classes that all need similarly structured Account and Contact test records. Which two practices reflect correct implementation of this pattern?", options: [("A", "Create a dedicated @isTest class (e.g., TestDataFactory) with static helper methods like createAccount(String name) that encapsulate record creation and insert logic, callable from any test class that needs this data."), ("B", "A test data factory class can only be used by exactly one test class; sharing it across multiple test classes violates test isolation principles."), ("C", "Any test class needing shared test data simply calls the factory's static methods, ensuring consistent test data structure and a single place to update when object fields or required values change."), ("D", "The test data factory class must never be marked with @isTest, since @isTest classes cannot contain reusable helper methods callable from other test classes."), ("E", "Test data factory methods should always use SeeAllData=true internally to guarantee realistic data characteristics matching production records.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Why correct: A dedicated @isTest utility class with static helper methods for record creation is the standard, correct test data factory pattern, and any test class can call these methods to get consistent, structured test data with a single point of maintenance when fields change — both accurately describe correct implementation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: Sharing a test data factory class across multiple test classes is the entire point of the pattern — it reduces duplication precisely by being reusable across many test classes, and this does not violate test isolation (each test method's data is still rolled back independently).  \n* D: @isTest classes absolutely can contain static helper methods callable from other test classes; this is precisely the standard, common implementation of the test data factory pattern, not a restriction against it.  \n* E: Test data factories should create their own isolated, deterministic test data rather than relying on SeeAllData=true, which introduces dependency on unpredictable real org data and violates test isolation principles."),
        Question(id: "91", question: "A developer needs to inspect the full state of every variable at a specific line of Apex code during a complex debugging session, beyond what scattered System.debug() statements conveniently reveal. What Developer Console feature is designed for this?", options: [("A", "The Query Editor, since it displays live variable state during Apex execution."), ("B", "The Test Runner tab, since it automatically captures full variable state for every line of code executed during any test run."), ("C", "The Checkpoint Inspector: set a checkpoint on a specific line in the gutter (up to 5 at a time), execute the code that reaches that line, then view a full heap dump snapshot of all variable values at that exact point in Checkpoint Inspector."), ("D", "Debug logs are the only mechanism available; checkpoints do not exist as a Developer Console feature.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: The Checkpoint Inspector is specifically designed for this exact need — setting a checkpoint on a line (up to 5 at once) captures a full heap dump snapshot of every variable's value at that point when execution reaches it, providing far more insight than scattered debug statements. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: The Query Editor is used for running SOQL/SOSL queries interactively and viewing results in a grid; it has no relationship to inspecting live Apex variable state during code execution.  \n* B: The Test Runner displays test execution results and coverage; it does not automatically capture a full variable-state heap dump for every line of executed code.  \n* D: Checkpoints are a real, documented Developer Console feature specifically for this heap-dump-inspection use case; claiming they don't exist is factually incorrect."),
        Question(id: "92", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to set up debug logging for a specific user experiencing an intermittent issue, and understands there is a tradeoff between log detail and log size. Which two facts about debug logs and log levels are accurate?", options: [("A", "Setting the log level to NONE captures the most comprehensive, most detailed log output possible."), ("B", "A trace flag must be set up in Setup > Debug Logs for the specific traced entity (user, class, or trigger) with an expiry time, in order to begin capturing logs for that user's future actions."), ("C", "Debug logs are retained indefinitely in Salesforce with no expiration, allowing historical review of logs from any point in the org's history."), ("D", "Log levels range from least to most verbose as: NONE, ERROR, WARN, INFO, DEBUG, FINE, FINER, FINEST; higher verbosity levels capture more detail but generate larger log files (up to 20 MB per log)."), ("E", "Trace flags apply globally and cannot be scoped to a specific user, class, or trigger.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Why correct: The documented log level sequence from least to most verbose is accurate, with higher verbosity producing larger logs (up to 20 MB), and setting up a trace flag for a specific traced entity with an expiry is exactly the correct, required setup process to begin capturing logs for a user's actions. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: NONE is the LEAST verbose setting, capturing no debug log detail at all; this option describes the exact opposite of NONE's actual behavior.  \n* C: Debug logs are retained for only 24 hours, not indefinitely; this is a well-documented, hard retention limit, not permanent historical storage.  \n* E: Trace flags are specifically designed to be scoped to a particular traced entity (a specific user, Apex class, or trigger); they are not purely global and unscoped."),
        Question(id: "93", question: "A developer needs to determine why a Batch Apex job silently failed for some chunks of records overnight, without having actively monitored it in real time. Where should they look, and what information is available?", options: [("A", "There is no way to review a Batch Apex job's execution history after the fact; monitoring must happen live as the job runs."), ("B", "Setup > Scheduled Jobs only, since Batch Apex jobs are exclusively tracked there and never appear in Apex Jobs."), ("C", "The Recycle Bin, since failed batch job chunks are automatically moved there for review."), ("D", "Setup > Apex Jobs, which lists all asynchronous Apex job executions with fields including Status (Queued, Processing, Completed, Failed, Aborted), JobType, MethodName, TotalJobItems, and NumberOfErrors — providing exactly the historical execution and failure information needed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Setup > Apex Jobs is specifically the location that logs all asynchronous Apex job executions (including Batch Apex) with status, job type, method name, total items, and error counts — exactly the historical information needed to diagnose an overnight batch failure after the fact. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Apex Jobs provides exactly this kind of after-the-fact historical review capability; live monitoring is not the only way to diagnose a batch job's outcome.  \n* B: Setup > Scheduled Jobs tracks scheduled (cron-based) job definitions and their next fire times; the actual execution history and per-chunk results of a Batch Apex run are tracked in Apex Jobs, not exclusively in Scheduled Jobs.  \n* C: The Recycle Bin holds deleted records; it has no relationship to Batch Apex job execution logs or failure information."),
        Question(id: "94", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to find and potentially abort a scheduled Apex job that appears to be running on an incorrect cron schedule, and wants to understand the SOQL-queryable objects involved. Which two facts are accurate?", options: [("A", "System.abortJob(cronTriggerId) is the correct Apex method to cancel a scheduled job programmatically, using the CronTrigger record's Id."), ("B", "CronJobDetail alone contains the NextFireTime field; CronTrigger has no scheduling-related fields at all."), ("C", "Aborting a scheduled job requires deleting the underlying Apex class that implements Schedulable, since jobs cannot be aborted independently of their class."), ("D", "Scheduled jobs can only be viewed and aborted through the Setup > Scheduled Jobs UI; there is no SOQL-queryable object representing them."), ("E", "Scheduled Apex jobs can be queried via SOQL using the CronTrigger object (e.g., SELECT Id, CronExpression, NextFireTime FROM CronTrigger), optionally joined with CronJobDetail for the job's name.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "Why correct: CronTrigger is the correct, SOQL-queryable object for scheduled job information (including CronExpression and NextFireTime, joinable with CronJobDetail for the name), and System.abortJob(cronTriggerId) is the correct, documented Apex method for programmatically cancelling a scheduled job using that CronTrigger Id. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* B: This reverses the actual field distribution — NextFireTime and CronExpression are fields on CronTrigger itself; CronJobDetail primarily provides the job's Name and related detail, not the scheduling fields.  \n* C: A scheduled job can be aborted independently via System.abortJob() or the Scheduled Jobs UI without needing to delete the underlying Schedulable class, which could still be used to schedule a new job later.  \n* D: CronTrigger is explicitly a SOQL-queryable object representing scheduled jobs; the Setup UI is not the only way to view or interact with this information."),
        Question(id: "95", question: "A developer is preparing to deploy Apex changes to production and needs to select the appropriate test level for the deployment. The change is a critical fix that must be validated against the entire org's existing Apex test suite (excluding managed package tests) before going live. Which test level is correct, and is it the default?", options: [("A", "RunSpecifiedTests — this is the default for all production deployments and requires no explicit test class list."), ("B", "RunLocalTests — runs all tests in the org not from installed managed packages; this is the DEFAULT test level applied for production deployments when no other test level is explicitly specified."), ("C", "NoTestRun — valid and commonly used for production deployments to speed up the release process."), ("D", "RunAllTestsInOrg is the default test level for every deployment, including to sandboxes and production alike.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: RunLocalTests runs every test in the org except those belonging to installed managed packages, and this is indeed the default test level Salesforce applies to production deployments when no other level is explicitly specified — exactly matching the described requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: RunSpecifiedTests requires the developer to explicitly list which test classes to run; it is not the default and does not run automatically without that explicit list.  \n* C: NoTestRun is only permitted for sandbox deployments; it is explicitly disallowed for production deployments, which always require some level of test execution and coverage.  \n* D: RunAllTestsInOrg (which includes managed package tests) is not the default for any deployment type; RunLocalTests is the production default, and sandbox deployments can even use NoTestRun."),
        Question(id: "96", question: "Choose 2. A developer is comparing sandbox types for an upcoming project needing realistic-but-not-full production data for functional testing, refreshed every few days as the team iterates. Which two facts about Partial Copy sandboxes are accurate?", options: [("A", "Partial Copy sandboxes never include any data at all, matching Developer sandboxes' metadata-only behavior."), ("B", "The minimum refresh interval for a Partial Copy sandbox is 5 days, aligning with a team that wants to refresh every few days as they iterate."), ("C", "Partial Copy sandboxes have the same 1-day minimum refresh interval as Developer sandboxes."), ("D", "Partial Copy sandboxes copy 100% of every object's production data with zero sampling or record limits."), ("E", "A Partial Copy sandbox includes a configurable sample of production data (via a sandbox template selecting specific objects, up to 10,000 records per object) rather than a full data copy.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4], explanation: "Why correct: A Partial Copy sandbox includes a configurable, template-driven sample of production data (up to 10,000 records per selected object, not a full copy), and its minimum refresh interval of 5 days aligns well with a team wanting to refresh every few days as they iterate. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Partial Copy sandboxes do include a genuine sample of actual production data (unlike Developer/Developer Pro sandboxes, which are metadata-only with zero data); claiming no data at all is incorrect.  \n* C: Developer sandboxes have a 1-day minimum refresh interval, but Partial Copy sandboxes have a longer, 5-day minimum refresh interval — these are not the same.  \n* D: This describes a Full sandbox's behavior, not Partial Copy; Partial Copy specifically samples data via a template rather than copying 100% of every object with no limits."),
        Question(id: "97", question: "A developer needs to deploy a new custom field along with a dependent Flow that references it, and wants to understand what's required in the package.xml manifest for a Metadata API deployment. What is correct?", options: [("A", "package.xml is optional for any Metadata API deployment; Salesforce automatically infers every component to deploy based on recently modified files with zero explicit manifest needed."), ("B", "package.xml must list each metadata component type and its members (e.g., <types><members>MyField__c</members><name>CustomField</name></types> and a similar block for the Flow), and can use <members>*</members> to include all components of a given type."), ("C", "A single package.xml file can only ever reference exactly one metadata type; deploying both a field and a Flow requires two entirely separate manifest files submitted independently."), ("D", "The <members>*</members> wildcard syntax is invalid in package.xml and will always cause a deployment failure.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: package.xml correctly requires listing each metadata type with its specific member components (or using the <members>*</members> wildcard to include all components of that type), and multiple different types (like CustomField and Flow) can and should be included as separate <types> blocks within the same single manifest file. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: package.xml is not optional for Metadata API-based deployments (like sf project deploy start using a manifest); it is the explicit manifest that drives what gets retrieved or deployed — there's no automatic inference replacing it in this workflow.  \n* C: A single package.xml file fully supports multiple <types> blocks for different metadata types (CustomField, Flow, ApexClass, etc.) all within one manifest; separate files per type are not required.  \n* D: The <members>*</members> wildcard is valid, documented syntax specifically meant to include all components of a given metadata type; it does not cause a deployment failure when used correctly."),
        Question(id: "98", question: "Choose 2. A developer needs to remove a deprecated custom field from a target org as part of a deployment, and is deciding between destructiveChanges.xml and destructiveChangesPre.xml. Which two facts correctly describe when to use each?", options: [("A", "destructiveChangesPre.xml and destructiveChanges.xml can never both be used in the same deployment; only one may be present at a time."), ("B", "The main package.xml accompanying a destructive changes deployment must always be non-empty and cannot be an empty manifest."), ("C", "destructiveChanges.xml deletes the specified components AFTER the deployment completes, appropriate when the deletion doesn't need to happen before the new metadata is in place."), ("D", "destructiveChangesPre.xml deletes the specified components BEFORE the rest of the deployment's metadata is deployed; use this when the components being deleted would otherwise conflict with new code being deployed in the same operation."), ("E", "Both destructiveChanges.xml and destructiveChangesPre.xml use a completely different XML schema/format from package.xml, requiring entirely different syntax to specify components.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3], explanation: "Why correct: destructiveChangesPre.xml correctly deletes components before the rest of the deployment (needed when deletions would conflict with incoming new metadata), while destructiveChanges.xml correctly deletes components after deployment completes (when deletion order relative to new metadata doesn't matter) — both statements accurately describe their distinct timing and use cases. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Both destructiveChangesPre.xml and destructiveChanges.xml can be used together in the same deployment operation when needed (e.g., deleting some components before, and others after, the main deployment).  \n* B: The accompanying package.xml can in fact be empty (or minimal) when a deployment consists solely of deletions with no new metadata being added; it is not required to be non-empty in every destructive-changes scenario.  \n* E: Both destructiveChanges.xml and destructiveChangesPre.xml use the exact same XML format/schema as package.xml (types, members, name elements); there is no different syntax required."),
        Question(id: "99", question: "A growing team of 12 developers working in parallel on the same Salesforce org is evaluating whether to continue using Change Sets or adopt a source-driven development model (Salesforce CLI plus Git-based version control). What consideration most strongly favors moving to source-driven development at this team size?", options: [("A", "Team size has no bearing on this decision; the two approaches are functionally interchangeable regardless of how many developers are working in parallel."), ("B", "Change Sets provide native version control history, branching, and merge conflict resolution that scale well for any team size, so there's no compelling reason for a large team to switch."), ("C", "Change Sets scale better than source-driven development specifically for teams larger than 10 developers, due to their built-in conflict resolution engine."), ("D", "Source-driven development provides genuine version history, branching/merging for parallel work streams, and repeatable, scriptable deployments — capabilities Change Sets fundamentally lack — which become increasingly valuable and often necessary as team size and deployment frequency grow.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: Source-driven development with Git provides real version history, branching and merging for parallel work, and scriptable, repeatable deployments — all capabilities Change Sets fundamentally lack — and these become progressively more valuable (often essential) as a team grows and deployment frequency increases, as in this 12-developer scenario. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: Team size and deployment frequency are significant, well-documented factors in choosing appropriate ALM tooling; claiming no bearing at all misrepresents standard best-practice guidance on this exact tradeoff.  \n* B: Change Sets have no native version control history, branching, or merge conflict resolution capability at all; this claim of scaling well for any team size directly contradicts their well-documented, fundamental limitations.  \n* C: Change Sets have no built-in conflict resolution engine whatsoever; this option describes a capability Change Sets simply do not have, making the claim about superior scaling factually baseless."),
        Question(id: "100", question: "A developer is deciding between an unmanaged package and a managed package for distributing a reusable utility component to multiple internal Salesforce orgs within the same company, where the receiving teams want to freely view, modify, and extend the Apex code after installation. Which packaging type is correct, and why?", options: [("A", "A managed package, since managed packages are always required for any package distributed to more than one org, regardless of whether code visibility is desired."), ("B", "Neither packaging type supports distributing Apex code to multiple orgs; this always requires manual copy-paste of code between orgs."), ("C", "An unmanaged package, since all components (including Apex code) remain fully visible and editable after installation, with no ongoing upgrade path required — appropriate for this scenario where the receiving teams specifically want to freely modify the code themselves."), ("D", "A managed package, since managed package Apex code becomes editable by installers once at least three orgs have installed it.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: An unmanaged package installs all components (including Apex) as fully visible, editable, unlocked metadata in each target org, which is exactly appropriate here since the receiving teams explicitly want to freely view, modify, and extend the code themselves after installation — with no need for a centralized upgrade mechanism. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n* A: There is no such requirement mandating managed packages simply because a package is distributed to more than one org; unmanaged packages are fully capable of being installed across multiple orgs.  \n* B: Both managed and unmanaged packages are legitimate, well-established mechanisms for distributing Apex code (and other metadata) to multiple orgs; manual copy-paste is not a required or standard distribution method.  \n* D: Managed package Apex code remains obfuscated/hidden from installers permanently; there is no threshold number of installing orgs after which the code becomes editable — this describes a nonexistent behavior.")
    ]
}
